ls and Biostatistics, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Callstrom MR","firstName":"Matthew R","lastName":"Callstrom","initials":"MR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Shah VH","firstName":"Vijay H","lastName":"Shah","initials":"VH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Garovic VD","firstName":"Vesna D","lastName":"Garovic","initials":"VD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN. Electronic address: garovic.vesna@mayo.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7308-1812"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4166742,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Mayo Clinic proceedings","medlineAbbreviation":"Mayo Clin Proc","isoabbreviation":"Mayo Clin Proc","nlmid":"0405543","essn":"1942-5546","issn":"0025-6196"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S0025-6196(26)18483-3","affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"UL1TR002377","agency":"National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"UL1 TR002377","agency":"NCATS NIH HHS","acronym":"TR","orderIn":0}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mayocp.2026.04.007"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13170483"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13170483?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mayocp.2026.04.007"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"Y","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"Y","manuscriptId":"NIHMS2168058","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-21"},{"id":"41977864","source":"MED","pmid":"41977864","pmcid":"PMC13074417","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13074417"]},"doi":"10.3390/s26072079","title":"A Systematic Review of Safety-Driven Approaches in Human-Robot Collaborative Systems.","authorString":"Khan A, Akhtar M, Qureshi SM, Mustafa M, Alsaleh NA, Ahmad I.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Khan A","firstName":"Akhtar","lastName":"Khan","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Electrical Engineering, Iqra Nations University, Peshawar 25000, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Akhtar M","firstName":"Maaz","lastName":"Akhtar","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5061-8090"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Industrial Engineering Department, College of Engineering, Imam Mohammad Ibn Saud Islamic University (IMSIU), Riyadh 11432, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Qureshi SM","firstName":"Sheheryar Mohsin","lastName":"Qureshi","initials":"SM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3728-9720"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Computing, Engineering and Physical Sciences, University of the West of Scotland, Paisley PA1 2BE, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Mustafa M","firstName":"Muzzamil","lastName":"Mustafa","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4896-0305"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Artificial Intelligence, University of Management and Technology, Lahore 54782, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Alsaleh NA","firstName":"Naser A","lastName":"Alsaleh","initials":"NA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5779-0451"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Industrial Engineering Department, College of Engineering, Imam Mohammad Ibn Saud Islamic University (IMSIU), Riyadh 11432, Saudi Arabia."}]}},{"fullName":"Ahmad I","firstName":"Imran","lastName":"Ahmad","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1931-3659"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Industrial Engineering, University of Engineering and Technology, Peshawar 25000, Pakistan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5779-0451"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3728-9720"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4896-0305"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5061-8090"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1931-3659"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"26","journalIssueId":4179674,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Sensors (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Sensors (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Sensors (Basel)","nlmid":"101204366","essn":"1424-8220","issn":"1424-8220"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2079","abstractText":"Collaboration between humans and robots (HRC) is advancing rapidly due to the intersection of robotics and generative artificial intelligence (GenAI). The current paper includes a systematic review of 103 studies on the role of generative models, including Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), diffusion models, and Large Language Models (LLMs) in improving the safety, trust, and adaptability of collaborative robotics using a PRISMA-based systematic approach. The review recognizes four major themed areas of GenAI-based safety frameworks-namely, data-driven simulation to synthesize hazards, predictive reasoning to forecast human motion, adaptive control to reduce risks dynamically, and trust-aware cognition to explain human-robot interaction. Findings indicate that generative models transform robotic safety from a reactive mechanism to proactive, contextual and interpretable systems. Nevertheless, real-time performance, interpretability, standard benchmarking, and ethical assurance are still some of the challenges to be overcome. The paper proposes a taxonomy linking generative modeling layers and physical, cognitive and ethical aspects of HRC safety, and gives a roadmap of certifiable hybrid systems with generative foresight and deterministic control. This synthesis provides a foundation for developing transparent, adaptive, and trustworthy collaborative robotic systems.","affiliation":"Department of Electrical Engineering, Iqra Nations University, Peshawar 25000, Pakistan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"IMSIU-DDRSP2602","agency":"This work was supported and funded by the Deanship of Scientific Research at Imam Mohammad Ibn Saud Islamic University (IMSIU)","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cooperative Behavior"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Safety"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Robotics","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Adversarial Networks"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Autoencoder"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Prisma","Generative Adversarial Networks","Generative Ai","Large Language Models","Variational Auto Encoders"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/s26072079"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13074417"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13074417?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-27"},{"id":"41485858","source":"MED","pmid":"41485858","pmcid":"PMC12965389","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12965389"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.mayocp.2025.11.008","title":"Building Artificial Intelligence and Data Literacy Across the Health Care Research Workforce in the Mayo Clinic Center for Clinical and Translational Science: Why It Matters and What We Are Doing About It.","authorString":"Zand J, Suvakov M, Overgaard SM, Kozikowski CG, Jimison RC, Nelson KB, Silvano CJ, Meiners LM, Fan JW, Schultz KE, Carter RE, Wieland ML, Patten CA, Dugani SB, Weis JA, Poe JD, Holmes DR, Griffiths LG, Dieter HL, Juhn YJ, Enders FT, Callstrom MR, Shah V, Meyer FB, Garovic VD.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zand J","firstName":"Jaleh","lastName":"Zand","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Suvakov M","firstName":"Milovan","lastName":"Suvakov","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Overgaard SM","firstName":"Shauna M","lastName":"Overgaard","initials":"SM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4494-0008"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Digital Health, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Kozikowski CG","firstName":"Chester G","lastName":"Kozikowski","initials":"CG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Jimison RC","firstName":"Ryan C","lastName":"Jimison","initials":"RC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-1931-7018"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Nelson KB","firstName":"Kristina B","lastName":"Nelson","initials":"KB","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7045-6414"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Silvano CJ","firstName":"Carmen J","lastName":"Silvano","initials":"CJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Meiners LM","firstName":"Laura M","lastName":"Meiners","initials":"LM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7308-1812"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Fan JW","firstName":"Jungwei W","lastName":"Fan","initials":"JW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Department of Artificial Intelligence and Informatics, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Schultz KE","firstName":"Kate E","lastName":"Schultz","initials":"KE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-8507-8373"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Digital Health, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Carter RE","firstName":"Rickey E","lastName":"Carter","initials":"RE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Wieland ML","firstName":"Mark L","lastName":"Wieland","initials":"ML","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Community Internal Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Patten CA","firstName":"Christi A","lastName":"Patten","initials":"CA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Psychology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Dugani SB","firstName":"Sagar B","lastName":"Dugani","initials":"SB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Division of Hospital Internal Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Weis JA","firstName":"Jenny A","lastName":"Weis","initials":"JA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Mayo Clinic Health System, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Poe JD","firstName":"John D","lastName":"Poe","initials":"JD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Education Administration, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Holmes DR 3rd","firstName":"David R","lastName":"Holmes","initials":"DR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physiology and Biomedical Engineering, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Griffiths LG","firstName":"Leigh G","lastName":"Griffiths","initials":"LG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cardiovascular Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Dieter HL","firstName":"Heidi L","lastName":"Dieter","initials":"HL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research Administration, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Juhn YJ","firstName":"Young J","lastName":"Juhn","initials":"YJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Department of Pediatric and Adolescent Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Enders FT","firstName":"Felicity T","lastName":"Enders","initials":"FT","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative Health Sciences, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Division of Clinical Trials and Biostatistics, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Callstrom MR","firstName":"Matthew R","lastName":"Callstrom","initials":"MR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Shah V","firstName":"Vijay","lastName":"Shah","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Meyer FB","firstName":"Fred B","lastName":"Meyer","initials":"FB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Neurologic Surgery, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN."}]}},{"fullName":"Garovic VD","firstName":"Vesna D","lastName":"Garovic","initials":"VD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; Center for Clinical and Translational Science, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN. Electronic address: garovic.vesna@mayo.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4494-0008"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7308-1812"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-8507-8373"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7045-6414"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-1931-7018"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"101","journalIssueId":4084538,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Mayo Clinic proceedings","medlineAbbreviation":"Mayo Clin Proc","isoabbreviation":"Mayo Clin Proc","nlmid":"0405543","essn":"1942-5546","issn":"0025-6196"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"17-25","affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"K23 MD016230","agency":"National Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"K23 MD016230","agency":"NIMHD NIH HHS","acronym":"MD","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"UL1 TR002377","agency":"NCATS NIH HHS","acronym":"TR","orderIn":0}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mayocp.2025.11.008"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12965389"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12965389?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mayocp.2025.11.008"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"Y","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"Y","manuscriptId":"NIHMS2147061","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-05","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},{"id":"42368301","source":"MED","pmid":"42368301","pmcid":"PMC13306875","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13306875"]},"doi":"10.35772/ghm.2026.01051","title":"Artificial intelligence (AI)-aided clinical data management: Applications, human-in-the-loop workflows, and regulatory considerations.","authorString":"Ohi S, Iwamoto T, Ikeda D, Kawanishi Y, Kitajima K, Ohyanagi H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ohi S","firstName":"Saya","lastName":"Ohi","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-8937-1279"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Bioinformatics, Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Iwamoto T","firstName":"Tomoko","lastName":"Iwamoto","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Clinical Data Management, Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Ikeda D","firstName":"Daiki","lastName":"Ikeda","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Bioinformatics, Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kawanishi Y","firstName":"Yuichi","lastName":"Kawanishi","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Bioinformatics, Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kitajima K","firstName":"Koji","lastName":"Kitajima","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Office of Clinical Data Management, Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Ohyanagi H","firstName":"Hajime","lastName":"Ohyanagi","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-8937-1279"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"8","journalIssueId":4222530,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Global health & medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"Glob Health Med","isoabbreviation":"Glob Health Med","nlmid":"101771579","essn":"2434-9194","issn":"2434-9186"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"182-192","abstractText":"Clinical data management (CDM) is central to the quality of clinical research. In Japan, CDM faces a shortage of qualified personnel, particularly in academic research organizations (AROs), as well as increasing data volume and complexity. Rapid advances in artificial intelligence (AI), especially large language models, have therefore attracted attention as a way to support CDM. This review summarizes domestic and international examples of AI utilization in CDM-related tasks, including data cleaning, medical coding, and query generation. Across the cases reviewed, a common implementation principle emerged: a human-in-the-loop design in which AI performs initial processing or detection, while final judgment remains with human personnel. This design is especially relevant to AROs, where high data quality must be maintained with limited CDM human resources. Regulatory frameworks, including ICH E6 (R3) and the FDA-EMA Guiding Principles, are beginning to address AI use, but how AI-aided processes should be handled under Good Clinical Practice remains under discussion. Comprehensive risk mitigation is therefore essential. AI and data are interdependent: better data improve AI performance, and better AI can further improve data quality. The shift from manual processes to human-AI collaborative workflows is likely to accelerate, and CDM must develop the technical, regulatory, and risk-management frameworks needed to support that transition.","affiliation":"Office of Bioinformatics, Department of Joint Center for Researchers, Associates and Clinicians (JCRAC), Center for Clinical Sciences, Japan Institute for Health Security, Tokyo, Japan.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial Intelligence (Ai)","Human-in-the-loop (Hitl)","Ai-related Regulations And Guidelines","Clinical Data Management (Cdm)"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.35772/ghm.2026.01051"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13306875"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13306875?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.35772/ghm.2026.01051"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"},{"id":"42500108","source":"MED","pmid":"42500108","doi":"10.1002/wjo2.70130","title":"Disparities in AI-Based Prior Authorization for Head and Neck Reconstruction: A Large Language Model Analysis.","authorString":"Wu SS, Shanmugam MV, Lee YJ, Ayoub NF.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Wu SS","firstName":"Shannon S","lastName":"Wu","initials":"SS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0809-9143"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery Stanford University School of Medicine Palo Alto California USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Shanmugam MV","firstName":"Mugil V","lastName":"Shanmugam","initials":"MV","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-0952-3556"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Quantitative and Computational Biology, Dornsife College of Letters, Arts, and Sciences University of Southern California Los Angeles California USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee YJ","firstName":"Yu-Jin","lastName":"Lee","initials":"YJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery Stanford University School of Medicine Palo Alto California USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ayoub NF","firstName":"Noel F","lastName":"Ayoub","initials":"NF","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1867-994X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery Stanford University School of Medicine Palo Alto California USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0809-9143"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1867-994X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-0952-3556"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4233489,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"World journal of otorhinolaryngology - head and neck surgery","medlineAbbreviation":"World J Otorhinolaryngol Head Neck Surg","isoabbreviation":"World J Otorhinolaryngol Head Neck Surg","nlmid":"101690857","essn":"2589-1081","issn":"2095-8811"}},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Large language models (LLM) are being rapidly integrated into healthcare, particularly to streamline time- and labor-intensive administrative processes. However, the potential for artificial intelligence (AI) systems to demonstrate bias when employed for insurance authorization remains poorly understood. As insurers increasingly adopt AI to make coverage decisions, this study examined bias in LLM-driven prior authorization in otolaryngology, using oral cavity squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) as a case study.<h4>Methods</h4>Using OpenAI's generative transformer GPT-4o, this study assessed for LLM bias when simulating insurance coverage decisions for head and neck cancer reconstruction. A standardized clinical scenario was constructed involving patients with T2N2 oral cavity SCC, all requiring surgical resection and reconstruction. The LLM was prompted to choose between a radial forearm free flap (RFFF) and split-thickness skin graft (STSG) for reconstruction, across 19,900 simulations. Patient profiles were systematically varied by age, sex, race/ethnicity, zip code-based income level, socioeconomic status (SES), and substance use history.<h4>Results</h4>The LLM output showed significant disparities in approval decisions. RFFF was more frequently approved for younger, Asian, or white patients from high-income zip codes or high SES backgrounds (<i>p</i> < 0.0001). Older, Black, and Hispanic patients, and those from lower-income areas or with substance use histories, were less likely to receive RFFF authorization (<i>p</i> < 0.0001). On sensitivity analysis, inclusion of tumor-specific information markedly skewed recommendations towards RFFF across sociodemographic backgrounds.<h4>Conclusion</h4>In this experimental study, the LLM's outputs exhibited significant disparities for oral cavity cancer reconstruction based on patient demographic variables in the setting of limited clinical information. Inputs to LLMs for clinical decision-making should include pertinent and detailed information to reduce the risk of bias. As insurers increasingly integrate AI for prior authorization, recognition of its biases, rigorous safeguards, and increased regulatory governance are needed to promote equitable health care.","affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery Stanford University School of Medicine Palo Alto California USA.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Health Insurance","Otolaryngology","Head And Neck Cancer","Prior Authorization","Large Language Model"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/wjo2.70130"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-26"},{"id":"42332966","source":"MED","pmid":"42332966","pmcid":"PMC13287375","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13287375"]},"doi":"10.2196/88536","title":"Blockchain-Based Dynamic and Revocable Consent for Secondary Health Data Use: Systematic Review.","authorString":"Phuyal S, Bhandari M, Bista R, Ferreira JC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Phuyal S","firstName":"Sudip","lastName":"Phuyal","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7524-1843"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Sciences, Technology and Architecture Research Center (ISTAR), Iscte - Instituto Universitário de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Bhandari M","firstName":"Manila","lastName":"Bhandari","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8354-581X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Sciences, Technology and Architecture Research Center (ISTAR), Iscte - Instituto Universitário de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Bista R","firstName":"Rabindra","lastName":"Bista","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0638-5840"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Kathmandu University, Dhulikhel, Nepal."}]}},{"fullName":"Ferreira JC","firstName":"João Carlos","lastName":"Ferreira","initials":"JC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6662-0806"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Sciences, Technology and Architecture Research Center (ISTAR), Iscte - Instituto Universitário de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Logistics, Molde University College, Britvegen 2, Molde, 6410, Norway, 47 71214000."},{"affiliation":"Inov Inesc, Lisbon, Portugal."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7524-1843"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0638-5840"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6662-0806"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8354-581X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4204633,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR medical informatics","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Med Inform","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Med Inform","nlmid":"101645109","essn":"2291-9694","issn":"2291-9694"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e88536","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>The secondary use of health data holds substantial potential for advancing biomedical research, strengthening population health analytics, and enabling artificial intelligence-driven decision-making support. Yet, ensuring that such reuse respects patient autonomy, privacy, and regulatory obligations remains a major challenge. Conventional consent mechanisms are typically static, difficult to revoke, and offer limited transparency or accountability after data disclosure.<h4>Objective</h4>This review aimed to systematically examine blockchain-based frameworks that enable dynamic, auditable, and revocable consent for the secondary use of health data.<h4>Methods</h4>A structured literature search was conducted in PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science covering the period 2020 to 2025. Following PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) guidelines, 55 peer-reviewed studies meeting predefined inclusion criteria were analyzed. Data extraction focused on four dimensions: (1) consent life cycle management, (2) auditability and traceability, (3) usability and patient empowerment, and (4) legal and ethical alignment.<h4>Results</h4>Findings indicate that blockchain technologies provide a robust foundation for automating consent life cycles, ensuring immutable auditability, and enabling decentralized patient control. Most frameworks used smart contracts, decentralized identifiers, and verifiable credentials to implement programmable and verifiable consent processes. Nevertheless, key challenges persist, including limited usability testing, complexities in real-time revocation propagation, interoperability gaps with clinical systems, and tensions with regulatory requirements such as the General Data Protection Regulation right to erasure. Only a small subset of studies reported real-world deployments or user-centered evaluations.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Blockchain offers substantial promise for improving the trustworthiness, transparency, and accountability of consent management for secondary health data use. However, wider adoption requires human-centered design approaches, stronger interoperability through standards such as Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources, verifiable credentials, and consent receipts, and clearer legal guidance for compliance. Future research should prioritize integrating blockchain-enabled consent infrastructures into national and cross-border digital health ecosystems such as the European Health Data Space to support secure, patient-controlled, and ethically governed secondary data use.","affiliation":"Information Sciences, Technology and Architecture Research Center (ISTAR), Iscte - Instituto Universitário de Lisboa, Lisbon, Portugal.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Confidentiality"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Informed Consent"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Blockchain"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Data Privacy","Dynamic Consent","Blockchain","Auditability","General Data Protection Regulation","Gdpr","Ehds","European Health Data Space","Health Care Data Sharing","Revocable Consent"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/88536"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13287375"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13287375?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-22"},{"id":"41930309","source":"MED","pmid":"41930309","pmcid":"PMC13040884","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13040884"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.esmorw.2025.100679","title":"How to bring generative AI to oncology practice.","authorString":"Truhn D, Kather JN.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Truhn D","firstName":"D","lastName":"Truhn","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Diagnostic and Interventional Radiology, University Hospital Aachen, Aachen, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Lab for AI in Medicine, University Hospital Aachen, Aachen, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Kather JN","firstName":"J N","lastName":"Kather","initials":"JN","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3730-5348"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Else Kroener Fresenius Center for Digital Health, Faculty of Medicine, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine I, Faculty of Medicine, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Medical Oncology, National Center for Tumor Diseases (NCT), University Hospital Heidelberg, Heidelberg, Germany."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3730-5348"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"11","journalIssueId":4133860,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"ESMO real world data and digital oncology","medlineAbbreviation":"ESMO Real World Data Digit Oncol","isoabbreviation":"ESMO Real World Data Digit Oncol","nlmid":"9919053637506676","essn":"2949-8201","issn":"2949-8201"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100679","abstractText":"Generative artificial intelligence (AI) is entering oncology. Large language models are the near-term workhorse because oncology runs on narrative text and structured tables. We review current adoption and outline a practical path from stand-alone chat models to retrieval-augmented systems and, ultimately, agentic assistants that plan tasks, call domain tools, and integrate multimodal data within the electronic health record. Concrete uses include molecular tumor board synthesis with transparent evidence, grading along guidelines, synoptic radiology and pathology drafting, and computable trial matching. We also map the constraints: fragmented hospital information technology, privacy and provenance requirements, domain shift across sites, and persistent hallucinations. We envision that evaluation must move beyond leaderboards toward multicenter, prospective designs with endpoints that reflect clinical utility, such as faithfulness to cited sources, extraction accuracy, time to task completion, plan correctness, recovery after tool failure, and silent clinical studies before exposure. Finally, we sketch an adoption trajectory. Institutions will replace <i>ad hoc</i> use of public tools with sanctioned drafting assistants, then embed retrieval and calculators inside the record, and only later enable event-driven agents that propose context-aware actions. The destination is augmentation, not automation: a learning assistant that shows its work, improves routine care, and leaves clinical judgment with clinicians.","affiliation":"Diagnostic and Interventional Radiology, University Hospital Aachen, Aachen, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"NIHR203331","agency":"National Institute for Health Research (NIHR)","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"101114631","agency":"European Research Council","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Oncology","Generative Ai","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.esmorw.2025.100679"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13040884"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13040884?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-03","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-03","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-30"},{"id":"42351930","source":"MED","pmid":"42351930","pmcid":"PMC13295611","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13295611"]},"doi":"10.3390/bioengineering13060686","title":"An Agentic LLM Framework for Autonomous Surgical Continuum Monitoring: ReAct-Driven Tool-Use Agents for Presurgical, Intraoperative, and Postsurgical Cardiopulmonary Care.","authorString":"Pylarinou C, Gortzis L, Leivaditis V, Liolis E, Antzoulas A, Papadoulas S, Nikolakopoulos K, Panagiotopoulos I, Mitsos S, Tomos P, Koletsis E, Mulita F.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pylarinou C","firstName":"Charalampia","lastName":"Pylarinou","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9421-9550"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Engineering & Biomedical Technology, University of Patras, 26500 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Gortzis L","firstName":"Lefteris","lastName":"Gortzis","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Leivaditis V","firstName":"Vasileios","lastName":"Leivaditis","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8692-0059"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiothoracic and Vascular Surgery, Westpfalz Klinikum, 67655 Kaiserslautern, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Liolis E","firstName":"Elias","lastName":"Liolis","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Oncology, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Antzoulas A","firstName":"Andreas","lastName":"Antzoulas","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Papadoulas S","firstName":"Spyros","lastName":"Papadoulas","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8628-2173"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Vascular Surgery, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Nikolakopoulos K","firstName":"Konstantinos","lastName":"Nikolakopoulos","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Vascular Surgery, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Panagiotopoulos I","firstName":"Ioannis","lastName":"Panagiotopoulos","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiac Surgery, Ippokrateio General Hospital of Athens, 11527 Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Mitsos S","firstName":"Sofoklis","lastName":"Mitsos","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0606-7287"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Attikon Hospital, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, 12462 Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Tomos P","firstName":"Periklis","lastName":"Tomos","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Attikon Hospital, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, 12462 Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Koletsis E","firstName":"Efstratios","lastName":"Koletsis","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1660-4047"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiothoracic Surgery, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Mulita F","firstName":"Francesk","lastName":"Mulita","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7198-2628"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7198-2628"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8628-2173"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8692-0059"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0606-7287"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9421-9550"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1660-4047"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"13","journalIssueId":4220338,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Bioengineering (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Bioengineering (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Bioengineering (Basel)","nlmid":"101676056","essn":"2306-5354","issn":"2306-5354"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"686","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Rule-based multi-agent system (MAS) architectures for healthcare coordination rely on hardcoded decision trees that cannot generalise to novel clinical scenarios or self-correct reasoning errors. These limitations are acute in surgical continuum care, where patients traverse presurgical risk stratification, intraoperative monitoring, postsurgical ICU, ward care, and remote rehabilitation over days to weeks-a complexity no fixed-policy agent architecture can address without prohibitive rule engineering.<h4>Objective</h4>We present the first agentic large language model (LLM) framework for autonomous end-to-end surgical continuum monitoring, superseding the prior rule-based MAS Digital Twin. Six ReAct-driven tool-use agents replace fixed-policy agents with dynamic reasoning, multi-hop evidence retrieval, and Reflexion self-correction while maintaining mandatory confidence-gated Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) gating at every care-pathway-modifying decision.<h4>Methods</h4>The framework is grounded in the ReAct paradigm and Reflexion self-evaluation, embedded within the DETER Digital Twin state engine S(t). Each agent is specified by a ReAct loop signature, a ten-function clinical tool registry, and confidence-gated HITL escalation logic. Inter-agent coordination replaces the rule-based Priority Queue Manager with an LLM-mediated Coordination Supervisor Agent reasoning over competing resource requests.<h4>Results</h4>The framework delivers: (i) six formally specified ReAct-loop agents with explicit tool registries and authorisation boundaries; (ii) a confidence-gated HITL architecture that reduces alert fatigue while preserving safety for ambiguous clinical scenarios; (iii) an extended conflict resolution function P(p,t,context) incorporating surgical phase and DETER deterioration trajectory gradient; (iv) Reflexion self-correction with a formal N_max = 2 termination condition and Clinical Factuality Verification Layer; and (v) a multi-phase Digital Twin state engine extending S(t) to the full surgical continuum.<h4>Conclusions</h4>The proposed framework represents a fundamental architectural departure from rule-based clinical AI-from hardcoded policies to dynamic reasoning, from static retrieval to multi-hop tool-use chains, and from fixed escalation thresholds to confidence-gated self-evaluation-providing a formally specified, clinically deployable foundation for next-generation autonomous surgical care coordination.","affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Engineering & Biomedical Technology, University of Patras, 26500 Patras, Greece.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Clinical Decision Support","Postsurgical Monitoring","Reflexion","Digital Twin","Hitl","Agentic Ai","Autonomous Healthcare Ai","Deter","React Framework","Surgical Continuum","Tool-Use Llm Agents"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/bioengineering13060686"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13295611"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13295611?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-15"},{"id":"42205634","source":"MED","pmid":"42205634","pmcid":"PMC13203309","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13203309"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.107729","title":"Detecting Citation Veneer in Guideline-Grounded Clinical and Public Health Large Language Model (LLM) Outputs: A Clinical Evidence Audit Grid Using CENHOV Tags.","authorString":"Harada Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Harada Y","firstName":"Yuusuke","lastName":"Harada","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Public Policy, Hosei University, Tokyo, JPN."},{"affiliation":"Humanities and Social Sciences, Hiroshima University, Hiroshima, JPN."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4172604,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e107729","abstractText":"Large language model (LLM) systems are increasingly used to summarize clinical guidelines, draft patient-facing education, and answer evidence-linked clinical or public health questions. We define citation veneer as an observable audit state in which an output presents citation cues or apparently supportive source information while still containing incorrect, incomplete, or materially unsupported content. We develop and evaluate the Clinical Evidence Audit Grid (CEAG), an analyst-facing monitoring surface that encodes six binary response-quality signals into a fixed-length plain-text audit tag (CENHOV): clinical correctness (C), evidential support (E), numeric concordance (N), hedging (H), stylistic ornamentation (O), and a derived CEAG-V supported-error citation veneer marker (V=1 when E=1 and C=0). CEAG was evaluated on a controlled benchmark (synthetic, n=120 questions per condition) and on a 12-case public check built from PubHealth-style health-claim examples and public medical or public-health sources. The controlled benchmark used scripted response regimes rather than a live LLM/API benchmark; therefore, model identity, sampling temperature, and seed are not applicable to the main controlled analysis. In the synthetic benchmark, the RUSH regime retained high evidential-support rates (93.3%, 95% CI 87.4%-96.6%) while dropping to 73.3% correctness (95% CI 64.8%-80.4%) and producing 25.8% CEAG-V veneer (95% CI 18.8%-34.3%). In the 12-case public check, RUSH produced 66.7% CEAG-V veneer (95% CI 39.1%-86.2%), whereas VERIFY and POSTER produced none; because n=12, these public-case rates are descriptive pilot data only. CEAG is not proposed as a patient-facing display or a replacement for conventional charts. It is a clinical informatics monitoring aid for reviewers who need to detect apparently well-sourced hallucinations, compare response regimes, and separate stylistic change from substantive trust change.","affiliation":"Public Policy, Hosei University, Tokyo, JPN.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Quality assurance","Patient education","Public Health","Patient Safety","Clinical Decision Support","Clinical Informatics","Large Language Models","Retrieval-augmented Generation","Citation Veneer","Hallucination Auditing"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.107729"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13203309"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13203309?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-26"},{"id":"42174156","source":"MED","pmid":"42174156","pmcid":"PMC13197458","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13197458"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02785-1","title":"Expert perspectives on the ecosystem of medical AI oversight in the GenAI era.","authorString":"Gilbert S, Dai T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Gilbert S","firstName":"Stephen","lastName":"Gilbert","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Else Kröner Fresenius Center for Digital Health, Faculty of Medicine, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany. stephen.gilbert@tu-dresden.de."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany. stephen.gilbert@tu-dresden.de."}]}},{"fullName":"Dai T","firstName":"Tinglong","lastName":"Dai","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Carey Business School, Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, MD, USA."},{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Johns Hopkins University, Baltimore, MD, USA."},{"affiliation":"Hopkins Business of Health Initiative, Johns Hopkins University, Washington, DC, USA."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4188236,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738","essn":"2398-6352","issn":"2398-6352"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"395","affiliation":"Else Kröner Fresenius Center for Digital Health, Faculty of Medicine, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany. stephen.gilbert@tu-dresden.de.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02785-1"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13197458"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13197458?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-22"},{"id":"41857339","source":"MED","pmid":"41857339","pmcid":"PMC13168469","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13168469"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02552-2","title":"Innovating global regulatory frameworks for generative AI in medical devices is an urgent priority.","authorString":"Ong JCL, Ning Y, Liu M, Ma Y, Zhao L, Singh K, Chang RT, Vogel S, Lim JCW, Tan ISK, Freyer O, Gilbert S, Bitterman DS, Liu X, Denniston AK, Liu N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ong JCL","firstName":"Jasmine Chiat Ling","lastName":"Ong","initials":"JCL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pharmacy, Singapore General Hospital, Singapore, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"Duke-NUS AI + Medical Sciences Initiative, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Ning Y","firstName":"Yilin","lastName":"Ning","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Duke-NUS AI + Medical Sciences Initiative, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"Centre for Biomedical Data Science, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu M","firstName":"Mingxuan","lastName":"Liu","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Duke-NUS AI + Medical Sciences Initiative, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"Centre for Biomedical Data Science, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Ma Y","firstName":"Yian","lastName":"Ma","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Duke-NUS AI + Medical Sciences Initiative, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"Centre for Biomedical Data Science, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhao L","firstName":"Liang","lastName":"Zhao","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bioengineering and Therapeutic Sciences, University of California, San Francisco, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Singh K","firstName":"Kuldev","lastName":"Singh","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, CA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Byers Eye Institute, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Chang RT","firstName":"Robert T","lastName":"Chang","initials":"RT","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, CA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Byers Eye Institute, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Vogel S","firstName":"Silke","lastName":"Vogel","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre of Regulatory Excellence, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Lim JCW","firstName":"John C W","lastName":"Lim","initials":"JCW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre of Regulatory Excellence, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Tan ISK","firstName":"Iris Siu Kwan","lastName":"Tan","initials":"ISK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence Office, Singapore Health Services, Singapore, Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Freyer O","firstName":"Oscar","lastName":"Freyer","initials":"O","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3323-2492"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Else Kröner Fresenius Center for Digital Health, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Gilbert S","firstName":"Stephen","lastName":"Gilbert","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1997-1689"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Else Kröner Fresenius Center for Digital Health, TUD Dresden University of Technology, Dresden, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Bitterman DS","firstName":"Danielle S","lastName":"Bitterman","initials":"DS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence in Medicine (AIM) Program, Mass General Brigham, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Brigham and Women's Hospital/Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu X","firstName":"Xiaoxuan","lastName":"Liu","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Medicine and Health, University of Birmingham, Birmingham, UK."},{"affiliation":"University Hospitals Birmingham NHS Foundation Trust, Birmingham, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Denniston AK","firstName":"Alastair K","lastName":"Denniston","initials":"AK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Medicine and Health, University of Birmingham, Birmingham, UK."},{"affiliation":"University Hospitals Birmingham NHS Foundation Trust, Birmingham, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu N","firstName":"Nan","lastName":"Liu","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Duke-NUS AI + Medical Sciences Initiative, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore. liu.nan@duke-nus.edu.sg."},{"affiliation":"Centre for Biomedical Data Science, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore. liu.nan@duke-nus.edu.sg."},{"affiliation":"Pre-hospital & Emergency Research Centre, Health Services Research & Population Health, Duke-NUS Medical School, Singapore, Singapore. liu.nan@duke-nus.edu.sg."},{"affiliation":"NUS Artificial Intelligence Institute, National University of Singapore, Singapore, Singapore. liu.nan@duke-nus.edu.sg."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics, Duke University, Durham, NC, USA. liu.nan@duke-nus.edu.sg."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1997-1689"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3323-2492"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3610-4883"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4136189,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738","essn":"2398-6352","issn":"2398-6352"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"364","abstractText":"The integration of generative AI (GenAI) and large language models (LLMs) in healthcare presents both unprecedented opportunities and challenges, necessitating innovative regulatory approaches. In this perspective, we discuss the risks of GenAI and LLM-based medical devices, the limitations of current medical device regulation frameworks when applied to GenAI or LLMs, and advocate for global collaboration in regulatory science research through engaging multidisciplinary expertise and focusing on the needs of diverse populations.","affiliation":"Division of Pharmacy, Singapore General Hospital, Singapore, Singapore.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02552-2"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13168469"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13168469?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":3,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-20","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-19","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-19"},{"id":"42072503","source":"MED","pmid":"42072503","pmcid":"PMC13114286","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13114286"]},"doi":"10.3390/e28040377","title":"Human-in-the-Loop Artificial Intelligence: A Systematic Review of Concepts, Methods, and Applications.","authorString":"Lazaros K, Vrahatis AG, Kotsiantis S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lazaros K","firstName":"Konstantinos","lastName":"Lazaros","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9527-3946"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Informatics, Ionian University, 49100 Corfu, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Vrahatis AG","firstName":"Aristidis G","lastName":"Vrahatis","initials":"AG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1892-0000"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Informatics, Ionian University, 49100 Corfu, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Kotsiantis S","firstName":"Sotiris","lastName":"Kotsiantis","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2247-3082"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Mathematics, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2247-3082"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9527-3946"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1892-0000"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"28","journalIssueId":4188730,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Entropy (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Entropy (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Entropy (Basel)","nlmid":"101243874","essn":"1099-4300","issn":"1099-4300"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"377","abstractText":"The integration of human judgment into artificial intelligence (AI) systems has emerged as a key research direction, particularly for high-stakes applications where full automation remains insufficient. Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) AI represents a field that combines machine learning capabilities with human oversight, feedback, and decision-making at various stages of the AI pipeline. This survey provides a systematic review of HITL approaches, covering theoretical foundations, technical methods, ethical considerations, and domain-specific applications. We propose a unified taxonomy that categorizes HITL systems based on loop placement, interaction granularity, and temporal characteristics. This review synthesizes findings from healthcare, autonomous systems, cybersecurity, and other high-risk domains where human oversight is essential. We also examine the challenges of scalability, cognitive load, and trust calibration that affect the practical deployment of HITL systems. The final section outlines open research directions and introduces a framework for designing effective human-AI collaborative systems.","affiliation":"Department of Informatics, Ionian University, 49100 Corfu, Greece.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Reinforcement Learning","Machine Learning","Active Learning","Human-in-the-loop","Explainable Ai","Human Oversight","Human–ai Collaboration"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/e28040377"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13114286"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13114286?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":4,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-26"},{"id":"41681707","source":"MED","pmid":"41681707","pmcid":"PMC12896711","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12896711"]},"doi":"10.3390/diagnostics16030390","title":"Large Language Models in Cardiovascular Prevention: A Narrative Review and Governance Framework.","authorString":"Ferreira Santos J, Dores H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ferreira Santos J","firstName":"José","lastName":"Ferreira Santos","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8690-3523"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Católica Medical School, Sintra Campus, Estrada Octávio Pato, 2635-631 Rio de Mouro, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Hospital da Luz Setubal, Luz Saúde, 2900-722 Setubal, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Dores H","firstName":"Hélder","lastName":"Dores","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4736-5221"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Hospital da Luz Lisboa, Luz Saúde, 1600-209 Lisbon, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"Associate Laboratory REAL, Comprehensive Health Research Center (CHRC), 1099-085 Lisbon, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"NOVA Medical School, NOVA University Lisbon, 1069-061 Lisbon, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"CoLab Trials, 7000-811 Évora, Portugal."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8690-3523"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4736-5221"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4120552,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Diagnostics (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Diagnostics (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Diagnostics (Basel)","nlmid":"101658402","essn":"2075-4418","issn":"2075-4418"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"390","abstractText":"<b>Background:</b> Large language models (LLMs) are becoming progressively integrated into clinical practice; however, their role in cardiovascular (CV) prevention remains unclear. This review synthesizes current evidence on LLM applications in preventive cardiology and proposes a governance framework for their safe translation into practice. <b>Methods:</b> We conducted a comprehensive narrative review of literature published between January 2015 and November 2025. Evidence was synthesized across three functional domains: (1) patient applications for health literacy and behavior change; (2) clinician applications for decision support and workflow efficiency; and (3) system applications for automated data extraction, registry construction, and quality surveillance. <b>Results:</b> Evidence suggests that while LLMs generate empathetic, guideline-concordant patient education, they lack the nuance required for unsupervised, personalized advice. For clinicians, LLMs effectively summarize clinical notes and draft documentation but remain unreliable for deterministic risk calculations and autonomous decision-making. System-facing applications demonstrate potential for automated phenotyping and multimodal risk prediction. However, safe deployment is constrained by hallucinations, temporal obsolescence, automation bias, and data privacy concerns. <b>Conclusions:</b> LLMs could help mitigate structural barriers in CV prevention but should presently be deployed only as supervised \"reasoning engines\" that augment, rather than replace, clinician judgment. To guide the transition from in silico performance to bedside practice, we propose the C.A.R.D.I.O. framework (Clinical validation, Auditability, Risk stratification, Data privacy, Integration, and Ongoing vigilance) as a roadmap for responsible integration.","affiliation":"Católica Medical School, Sintra Campus, Estrada Octávio Pato, 2635-631 Rio de Mouro, Portugal.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Risk stratification","Clinical Decision Support","Cardiovascular Prevention","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics16030390"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12896711"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12896711?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1129723","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"CrossRef Pre-print loader","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-02-16","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-26"},{"id":"42591666","source":"MED","pmid":"42591666","doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1870819","title":"Continuous assurance for AI-driven clinical decision support systems.","authorString":"Al-Horani RA, Tadesse AF.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Al-Horani RA","firstName":"Rami A","lastName":"Al-Horani","initials":"RA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Pharmaceutical Sciences, College of Pharmacy, Xavier University of Louisiana, New Orleans, LA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Tadesse AF","firstName":"Amanuel F","lastName":"Tadesse","initials":"AF","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Accounting, LSU New Orleans, Henry Bernstein College of Business Administration, New Orleans, LA, United States."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1870819","abstractText":"Healthcare systems are rapidly embedding adaptive and generative AI into core clinical processes. The integration of Artificial Intelligence into Clinical Decision Support Systems (AI-CDSS) highlights a fundamental transformation within healthcare delivery. This transformation enables advanced predictive analytics, multimodal data integration, and real-time augmentation of clinical decisions. However, AI introduces systemic, ethical, operational, and governance risks that challenge traditional healthcare audit and assurance frameworks. In fact, assurance methodologies designed for static software are becoming insufficient for patient safety and regulatory compliance. This review discusses the evolving landscape of AI-CDSS audit, highlighting its transition from a technically focused lifecycle validation to an integrated paradigm centered on socio-technical resilience. Early audit approaches adapted conventional medical device and software validation models to machine learning-enabled clinical tools. While these models established baseline safety oversight, they were not designed to address adaptive algorithms functioning in complex, evolving clinical environments. Modern governance frameworks emphasize continuous performance monitoring, lifecycle surveillance, and structured human oversight. However, a persistent implementation gap remains. Many audit models poorly capture the interactions among algorithmic behavior, clinical workflows, organizational culture, and shifting patient populations. In fact, three major paradigm shifts are reforming the AI-CDSS audit. First, the audit scope is expanding from model-centric evaluation to ecosystem-level assurance that incorporates workflow integration, human-machine collaboration quality, and organizational learning capacity. Second, the field is moving from post-hoc explainability toward reasoning traceability and synergistic clinical sense-making. Third, audit philosophy is shifting from static compliance verification toward resilience-oriented monitoring that focuses on adaptive capacity, graceful degradation, and safe performance evolution. Thus, we propose the STRAICS framework (Socio-Technical Resilience Assurance for Intelligent Clinical Systems), which integrates technical robustness, human-machine interaction safeguards, adaptive governance, and transparency-by-design infrastructure. These components are vital for building trustworthy, effective, and impartial clinical AI ecosystems that can safely manage the increasing complexity of healthcare.","affiliation":"Division of Pharmaceutical Sciences, College of Pharmacy, Xavier University of Louisiana, New Orleans, LA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"R16 GM149412","agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Collaborative Decision Making","Healthcare Artificial Intelligence","Continuous Assurance","Straics Framework","Socio-technical Resilience"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1870819"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-15","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-15","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-29"},{"id":"42330487","source":"MED","pmid":"42330487","pmcid":"PMC13286327","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13286327"]},"doi":"10.2196/94972","title":"Solidarity or Segregation? ChatGPT Health and US Health Care Disparities.","authorString":"Barnhart AJ, Comerci G, Prainsack B, Braun M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Barnhart AJ","firstName":"Andrew J","lastName":"Barnhart","initials":"AJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7935-253X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Social Ethics, University of Bonn, Am Hofgarten 8, Bonn, North Rhine-Westphalia, 53113, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Comerci G","firstName":"Giuseppe","lastName":"Comerci","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-2520-082X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Social Ethics, University of Bonn, Am Hofgarten 8, Bonn, North Rhine-Westphalia, 53113, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Prainsack B","firstName":"Barbara","lastName":"Prainsack","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6335-1532"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Political Science, University of Vienna, Vienna, Austria."}]}},{"fullName":"Braun M","firstName":"Matthias","lastName":"Braun","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6687-6027"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Social Ethics, University of Bonn, Am Hofgarten 8, Bonn, North Rhine-Westphalia, 53113, Germany."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6335-1532"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6687-6027"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7935-253X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-2520-082X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e94972","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>ChatGPT Health, an artificial intelligence feature launched by OpenAI in January 2026, integrates personal medical records and consumer health data into a large language model-based chatbot. It positions itself as a digital health tool to help users navigate a fragmented US health care system marked by inaccessible insurance, medical deserts, and workforce shortages. We argue that, rather than closing these gaps, ChatGPT Health risks widening what we call the solidarity gap in health care. By appearing to address unmet needs, it may obscure and entrench the structural conditions that produce health care disparities. We identify specific risks to patient safety, such as the legitimization of dangerous self-rationing and self-medication behaviors, inaccurate triage of clinical emergencies, erosion of effective health communication, and the reinforcement of confirmation and anchoring bias. These risks are particularly acute for individuals facing structural barriers to care. To prevent ChatGPT Health and similar large language model-based health tools from deepening inequities, we propose solidarity-based policy interventions such as upstream reforms that rebuild the institutional foundations of access and health equity and downstream safeguards that embed artificial intelligence tools within accountable, equitable health care infrastructures as complements to rather than substitutes for human care.","affiliation":"Department of Social Ethics, University of Bonn, Am Hofgarten 8, Bonn, North Rhine-Westphalia, 53113, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"101076822","agency":"European Research Council","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"United States"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Healthcare Disparities"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","AI","Solidarity","Health Care Inequity","Digital Health Policy","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/94972"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13286327"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13286327?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-22"},{"id":"42269012","source":"MED","pmid":"42269012","pmcid":"PMC13252702","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13252702"]},"doi":"10.2196/93470","title":"Ethical Governance of Large Language Models in Health Care: Trust, Responsibility, and Equity in Routine Use.","authorString":"Yang X, Xu C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yang X","firstName":"Xiongwen","lastName":"Yang","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4968-8953"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, 83 Zhongshan East Road, Guiyang, Guizhou, 550002, China, 86 18620726507, 86 18620726507."},{"affiliation":"NHC Key Laboratory of Pulmonary Immunological Diseases, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, Guiyang, Guizhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu C","firstName":"Chuan","lastName":"Xu","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-5849-8780"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, 83 Zhongshan East Road, Guiyang, Guizhou, 550002, China, 86 18620726507, 86 18620726507."},{"affiliation":"NHC Key Laboratory of Pulmonary Immunological Diseases, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, Guiyang, Guizhou, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4968-8953"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-5849-8780"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e93470","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>Large language models (LLMs) are becoming increasingly embedded in routine health care communication, raising ethical challenges that extend beyond model performance alone. This Viewpoint argues that ethical risks in LLM-enabled health care emerge through patterns of reliance, institutional embedding, and governance during real-world use. Using \"adoption-phase ethics\" as an analytic lens, this paper examines 3 interrelated dimensions of ethical risk. First, trust in LLM-enabled health care is shaped not only by technical accuracy, but also by institutional and relational conditions surrounding its use. Second, responsibility may become distributed and ambiguous when LLM-mediated information influences clinical communication and decision-making. Third, equity concerns arise from unequal capacities to interpret, contest, and benefit from LLM-generated information. We argue that ethical governance of LLMs in health care requires continuous, system-level oversight that extends beyond model evaluation alone, including clear accountability structures, role-sensitive implementation, and equity-oriented governance practices. By reframing ethical analysis around routine integration rather than technical performance alone, this Viewpoint aims to support more responsible and sustainable use of LLMs in health care.","affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, 83 Zhongshan East Road, Guiyang, Guizhou, 550002, China, 86 18620726507, 86 18620726507.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Trust"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Social Responsibility"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Delivery of Health Care","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Medical Ethics","Responsibility","Technology Adoption","Health Care Communication","Ethical Governance","Large Language Models","Equity And Trust"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/93470"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13252702"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13252702?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-11","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-10"},{"id":"42320966","source":"MED","pmid":"42320966","pmcid":"PMC13289074","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13289074"]},"doi":"10.1136/bmjopen-2025-111656","title":"Local performance and fairness testing of an AI Scribe in a paediatric developmental assessment clinic in South Australia: a silent trial protocol.","authorString":"McCradden MD, Tng S, Jeyaseelan D, Leane C, Campbell M, Braund T, Earle-Bandaralage L, Ebrahimi M, Sharma A, Tang J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"McCradden MD","firstName":"Melissa D","lastName":"McCradden","initials":"MD","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6476-2165"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Women's and Children's Health Network, North Adelaide, South Australia, Australia melissa.mccradden@adelaide.edu.au."},{"affiliation":"Australian Institute for Machine Learning, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."},{"affiliation":"SickKids Research Institute, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Tng S","firstName":"Sheng","lastName":"Tng","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Women's and Children's Health Network, North Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Jeyaseelan D","firstName":"Deepa","lastName":"Jeyaseelan","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Women's and Children's Health Network, North Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Leane C","firstName":"Cathy","lastName":"Leane","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Aboriginal Health and Wellbeing Division, Women's and Children's Health Network, North Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Campbell M","firstName":"Marnie","lastName":"Campbell","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Aboriginal Health and Wellbeing Division, Women's and Children's Health Network, North Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Braund T","firstName":"Timothy","lastName":"Braund","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Consumer representative, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Earle-Bandaralage L","firstName":"Lana","lastName":"Earle-Bandaralage","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Consumer representative, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Ebrahimi M","firstName":"Mary","lastName":"Ebrahimi","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Consumer representative, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Sharma A","firstName":"Ashish","lastName":"Sharma","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Consumer representative, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Tang J","firstName":"Jonathan","lastName":"Tang","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Women's and Children's Health Network, North Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6476-2165"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4204776,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"BMJ open","medlineAbbreviation":"BMJ Open","isoabbreviation":"BMJ Open","nlmid":"101552874","essn":"2044-6055","issn":"2044-6055"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e111656","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Any tool that can reduce the administrative burden on healthcare providers while preserving safe, accountable and high-quality medical documentation is of immense value both to healthcare institutions and consumers. The key question we need to answer is whether a prospective tool can reduce these burdens while maintaining (and, ideally <i>elevating</i>) quality documentation standards. The goal of this study is to describe the local performance of a large language model-based documentation assistive tool to draft safe, high-quality documentation in the Child Development Unit at the Women's and Children's Hospital. By generating local evidence of performance, we can assess the suitability of the artificial intelligence (AI) Scribe and inform a larger interventional study protocol and establish evidence-based governance.<h4>Methods and analysis</h4>Using an algorithmic audit framework developed specific to our context, we will compare clinician-written clinical notes to AI-generated notes produced in parallel to the standard of care (ie, a 'silent' or translational trial paradigm). We will compare the time required to review clinical documentation per the standard of care compared with the AI-supported workflow with consideration to the accuracy of the final documentation. Finally, we will qualitatively describe AI-generated notes and compare them to the current standard to identify specific areas where clinical guidelines (eg, performance information, risk mitigation) would support appropriate clinical use.<h4>Ethics and dissemination</h4>Ethics approval has been obtained by the Women's and Children's Health Network Human Research Ethics Committee (HREC) (HRE00067) and the South Australian Aboriginal HREC (#04-25-1185). This protocol offers an accessible example for health institutions looking to apply an evidence-based approach to AI Scribe assessment that prioritises clinical documentation standards. We will publish our study results in an academic journal and include a publicly accessible summary for the general public on the Women's and Children's website.<h4>Trial registration number</h4>10.17605/OSF.IO/P6TM5.","affiliation":"Women's and Children's Health Network, North Adelaide, South Australia, Australia melissa.mccradden@adelaide.edu.au.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Child Development"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Pediatrics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Research Design"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Documentation","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Child"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"South Australia"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Clinical Audit","Health Informatics","Protocols & Guidelines","Health Equity","Developmental Neurology & Neurodisability"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2025-111656"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13289074"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13289074?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-20","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-19","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-19"},{"id":"41606555","source":"MED","pmid":"41606555","pmcid":"PMC12924558","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12924558"]},"doi":"10.1186/s12889-025-26148-9","title":"Generative AI and health misinformation: production, propagation, and mitigation-a systematic review.","authorString":"Saeidnia HR, Jahani S, Ghiasi N, Keshavarz H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Saeidnia HR","firstName":"Hamid Reza","lastName":"Saeidnia","initials":"HR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Knowledge and Information Science, Tarbiat Modares University, (TMU), Tehran, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Jahani S","firstName":"Shamim","lastName":"Jahani","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research Committee, Babol University of Medical Sciences, Babol, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Ghiasi N","firstName":"Nasrin","lastName":"Ghiasi","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Public Health, School of Health, Ilam University of Medical Sciences, Ilam, Iran. ghnazi93@gmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Keshavarz H","firstName":"Hamid","lastName":"Keshavarz","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Knowledge and Information Science, Shahid Beheshti University, Tehran, Iran."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"26","journalIssueId":4082921,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"BMC public health","medlineAbbreviation":"BMC Public Health","isoabbreviation":"BMC Public Health","nlmid":"100968562","essn":"1471-2458","issn":"1471-2458"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"693","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Generative artificial intelligence technologies have disrupted information ecosystems, posing new threats to public health by enabling rapid, scalable manufacture of convincing but false health stories. This systematic review synthesizes evidence on how generative AI reconfigures health misinformation creation, dissemination, and moderation.<h4>Methods</h4>In line with PRISMA 2020, 15 empirical studies published between January 2023 and August 2025 were included. Databases consulted were MEDLINE (via PubMed), Embase, Scopus, Web of Science Core Collection, ACM Digital Library, IEEE Xplore, PsycINFO, Communication & Mass Media Complete, arXiv, and medRxiv/SSRN. Studies were contrasted on the basis of production capacity, propagation dynamics, and efficacy of mitigation at technical, sociotechnical, and governance layers.<h4>Results</h4>The synthesis indicates that generative AI substantially increases the volume, speed, and perceived credibility of health disinformation production, while altering its propagation dynamics. Users often struggle to distinguish AI‑generated from human‑authored health misinformation, and their sharing intentions are not tightly coupled with perceived accuracy. Existing detection systems show limited performance against AI‑generated content, and while labeling interventions can reduce perceived accuracy, their effects are context‑dependent.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Generative AI transforms the health misinformation landscape by lowering barriers to creation and exploiting platform and behavioral dynamics. Current mitigation strategies-spanning technical, sociotechnical, and governance layers-are promising but remain nascent and unevenly evaluated. Future work must prioritize multimodal, multilingual, and health‑specific verification, as well as real‑world testing of interventions, to build equitable and resilient health information ecosystems.","affiliation":"Department of Knowledge and Information Science, Tarbiat Modares University, (TMU), Tehran, Iran.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Communication"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Information Dissemination","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Health Communication"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Systematic review","Mitigation Strategies","Health Misinformation","Deepfakes","Generative Ai"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12889-025-26148-9"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12924558"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12924558?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-06","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-29"},{"id":"PPR1278866","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-10329560/v1","title":"Structural Reallocation in Finance: The Tri-Faceted Impact of AI on Securities, Wealth Management, and Algorithmic Governance","authorString":"Goyal A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Goyal A","firstName":"Aarav","lastName":"Goyal","initials":"A"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>This paper investigates the impacts of generative AI and machine learning upon the labor markets within the financial services industry. Unlike many economic analyses of AI and ML that consider the effects upon different economic sectors and industries as a whole, this paper considers the impacts of these technologies upon different sub-sectors within the financial services industry. In this way, the paper reveals how AI has led to the reallocation of labor within the financial industry, rather than its displacement of labor. In the sub-sector that relates to securities and implementing machine learning models to perform various mathematical tasks within trading, AI has acted as a substitute for labor within that sub-sector, leading to the elimination of the positions of junior analysts, and leading to a growing gap in the training of individuals to fill those positions. In financial consulting and wealth management, however, AI has acted as an augmenting technology for those sub-sectors, enabling analysts to dedicate more of their time to higher-priced tasks that require the human judgment of an expert financial professional. Furthermore, various studies have revealed that the deployment of these AI and ML models within financial services and trading has created a growing need for a third sub-sector within the financial industry: a Risk Management sub-sector that is responsible for deploying AI to monitor and audit the performance of these autonomous trading and risk management models. Finally, through building and simulating a model of the financial industry that incorporates these three sub-sectors, conducting a Difference-in-Differences analysis on the simulated industry to evaluate the labor market shifts that should occur in response to the releases of various generative AI and ML models, the article reveals the impacts that AI and ML are having upon the financial industry and its labor markets. The results of that model are presented as an illustrative example of the impacts of AI upon the financial markets, rather than as estimates based upon the labor markets of financial companies. Furthermore, in proposing that AI has created a gap in the training and development of individuals that can act as analysts for financial trading models, the article proposes that such an impact can be evaluated through establishing AI as an externality in the training of financial analysts, leading to the revelation that such an externality can lead to the under-supply of those analysts. Thus, the article concludes that the changes required within the financial industry in response to the integration and deployment of AI and ML models will require a restructuring of the type of individuals that populate the financial industry: those with expertise in both technology and financial analysis.</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-10329560/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1278866","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-07-14","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-14"},{"id":"42453683","source":"MED","pmid":"42453683","doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1849190","title":"From &lt;b&gt;\"&lt;/b&gt;assistant\" to &lt;b&gt;\"&lt;/b&gt;autonomous\": legal liability and ethical traceability frameworks for generative AI in clinical misdiagnosis scenarios, with a special focus on paediatrics.","authorString":"Xu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Xu S","firstName":"Shiyi","lastName":"Xu","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Capital Medical University Second Clinical School, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1849190","abstractText":"The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into clinical workflows has blurred the boundary between passive decision-support tools and systems capable of autonomous diagnostic reasoning. Current regulatory instruments-including the US FDA Clinical Decision Support Software (CDSS) guidance and the EU Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation EU 2024/1689)-classify these systems primarily as \"decision support,\" yet the non-deterministic outputs and emergent reasoning of generative AI introduce liability complexities these frameworks were not designed to address. This hypothesis and theory article examines the regulatory gap surrounding LLM-associated clinical errors. Drawing on regulatory precedents, case law, and a representative scenario of polypharmacy mismanagement, we analyze the risks of automation bias and the structural inadequacy of the Learned Intermediary Doctrine when applied to generative AI. We advance the autonomy-liability correspondence hypothesis: legal liability should be allocated as a monotonic function of a system's structural autonomy from meaningful human review. From this we derive a \"Three-Tiered Liability Escalation Framework\" (T-LEF) that allocates accountability among AI developers, healthcare institutions, and clinicians according to measurable degrees of autonomy. Arguing that traditional Explainable AI is technically insufficient for foundation models, we propose Clinical Algorithmic Audit Trails (CAAT)-a cryptographically secured, privacy-preserving traceability infrastructure-as a complement to explainability, uncertainty estimation, human-factors evaluation, and post-market surveillance, alongside a structured Safe Harbor provision to support compliant innovation. Because paediatric patients-from neonates to adolescents-constitute a population in which these failure modes are both more probable and more consequential, we give paediatric care explicit focus: under-representation of paediatric data in training corpora, weight- and development-based dosing across narrow therapeutic windows, and children's inability to detect or contest erroneous recommendations jointly intensify these concerns, and a dedicated section examines how the T-LEF, the Clinically Deceptive Hallucination (CDH) evidentiary test, and CAAT apply to paediatric deployment. We acknowledge that the T-LEF and CAAT remain prospective proposals requiring empirical validation, stakeholder consultation, and refinement before adoption. This framework is offered as a structured contribution to regulatory deliberation, not a finalized implementation blueprint.","affiliation":"Capital Medical University Second Clinical School, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Medical Liability","Paediatrics","Health Policy","Clinical Decision Support","Artificial Intelligence Ethics","Generative Ai","Paediatric Patient Safety"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1849190"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-16","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-30"},{"id":"42531993","source":"MED","pmid":"42531993","doi":"10.1016/j.forsciint.2026.113074","title":"Integrating generative AI into forensic workflows: A case study from the Netherlands Forensic Institute.","authorString":"Meconi TNT, Henseler H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Meconi TNT","firstName":"Timo N T","lastName":"Meconi","initials":"TNT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-5289-4157"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Netherlands Forensic Institute, Laan van Ypenburg 6, 2497 GB The Hague, The Netherlands; Security, Technology & e-Privacy, University of Groningen, The Netherlands. Electronic address: t.n.t.meconi@rug.nl."}]}},{"fullName":"Henseler H","firstName":"Hans","lastName":"Henseler","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Netherlands Forensic Institute, Laan van Ypenburg 6, 2497 GB The Hague, The Netherlands; Digital Forensics & E-Discovery, University of Applied Sciences Leiden, The Netherlands. Electronic address: henseler.h@hsleiden.nl."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-5289-4157"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"388","journalIssueId":4229670,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"Forensic science international","medlineAbbreviation":"Forensic Sci Int","isoabbreviation":"Forensic Sci Int","nlmid":"7902034","essn":"1872-6283","issn":"0379-0738"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"113074","abstractText":"Large Language Models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT promise efficiency gains in routine text-based work, but their use in forensic settings raises challenges for confidentiality, governance, and forensic validation. Prior studies have evaluated LLMs on single tasks within single forensic disciplines, leaving open the question under which conditions such models can be embedded in the day-to-day workflows of a forensic institute. This paper reports a three-month pilot at the Netherlands Forensic Institute, in which 50 forensic and support staff used Robin, a ChatGPT-4o instance hosted in a Trusted Cloud environment of the Dutch Ministry of Justice and Security, governed by a Data Protection Impact Assessment and a GDPR-compliant processing agreement. We report user-experienced applications gathered from semi-structured interviews, walk-in sessions, and follow-up mini-projects, and group them into three recurring categories: generating new text, reducing larger inputs to relevant information, and transforming text between styles, languages, or representations. Participants found Robin most useful for low-risk writing support, while expectations quickly extended towards system integration, document-grounded retrieval, coding assistance, and case-oriented support. We also discuss the technical, organisational, ethical, and legal conditions for sustainable deployment, and argue that workflow support and forensic reasoning require different levels of human oversight and discipline-specific validation. The pilot is presented as an exploratory institutional case study rather than as a performance evaluation of an LLM for forensic analysis.","affiliation":"Netherlands Forensic Institute, Laan van Ypenburg 6, 2497 GB The Hague, The Netherlands; Security, Technology & e-Privacy, University of Groningen, The Netherlands. Electronic address: t.n.t.meconi@rug.nl.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Ai Assistance","Ai Governance","Generative Ai","Large Language Models","Chatgpt","Forensic Ai"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.forsciint.2026.113074"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-30","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-31","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-30","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-28","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-28"},{"id":"41818744","source":"MED","pmid":"41818744","pmcid":"PMC13022535","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13022535"]},"doi":"10.2196/87830","title":"Trustworthy AI-Augmented Objective Structured Clinical Examinations in Nursing Education: Taiwan-Japan Viewpoint on 5 AI Roles, Governance, and Cross-Border Implementation.","authorString":"Kubota K, Nishimura A, Seto R, Li L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kubota K","firstName":"Kazumi","lastName":"Kubota","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2270-2313"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research Organization, Shimonoseki City University, Shimonoseki, Yamaguchi, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Healthcare Information Management, University of Tokyo Hospital, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Nishimura A","firstName":"Ayako","lastName":"Nishimura","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6981-1667"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Healthcare, Division of Nursing, Tokyo Healthcare University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Seto R","firstName":"Ryoma","lastName":"Seto","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6154-9342"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Healthcare, Division of Healthcare Informatics, Tokyo Healthcare University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Li L","firstName":"Liu","lastName":"Li","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4800-644X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Show Chwan Health Care System, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4800-644X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6154-9342"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6981-1667"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2270-2313"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"10","journalIssueId":4136778,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR formative research","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","nlmid":"101726394","essn":"2561-326X","issn":"2561-326X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e87830","abstractText":"Generative artificial intelligence (AI) is arriving in high-stakes assessment; however, governance, validity evidence, and faculty readiness remain uneven. From a Taiwan-Japan perspective, we outline a pragmatic, transferable approach to integrating AI into nursing objective structured clinical examinations (OSCEs) using a 5-AI-role model-learning assistant, AI‑augmented standardized patient, assessment assistant, case generator, and learning analyst-mapped across pre-OSCE, peri-OSCE, and post-OSCE workflows with human-in-the-loop final judgment. Taiwan contributes agile interdisciplinary development, staged pilots (practice, mock OSCE, and limited high-stakes stations), A/B comparisons, and explainability-by-design logging that links scores to time-stamped evidence. Japan contributes robust policy scaffolding (national AI use guidance in K-12, a revised nursing model core curriculum with outcomes and assessment blueprints, and institutional research cultures that support auditability and quality assurance). We distill 4 cross-cutting governance pillars-human oversight, learning process transparency, ethics and safety, and traceability-into implementable techniques (machine-readable rubrics, standardized patient persona cards, bias monitoring, and targeted faculty development). Aligning with international principles (International Advisory Committee for AI; Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development; United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization; World Health Organization; European Commission's High Level Expert Group; and National Institute of Standards and Technology), we propose a joint road map and shared registry to benchmark reliability, validity, equity, and workload impact. This viewpoint targets OSCE directors, nursing educators, and institutional leaders and provides a phase-gated governance blueprint rather than reporting original trial outcomes. Taiwan-led agility, complemented by Japan's standards-driven assurance, can form an Asia-Pacific reference model for trustworthy AI‑augmented OSCE in nursing education.","affiliation":"Research Organization, Shimonoseki City University, Shimonoseki, Yamaguchi, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Education, Nursing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Educational Measurement","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Japan"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Taiwan"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","AI","Nursing Education","Standardized Patient","Osce","Objective Structured Clinical Examination","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Generative Ai","Taiwan-japan Collaboration","Assessment Governance","Transparency And Traceability"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/87830"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13022535"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13022535?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-11","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-12"},{"id":"42147040","source":"MED","pmid":"42147040","pmcid":"PMC13176136","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13176136"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1800047","title":"The pediatric AI readiness framework: bridging evidence to practice in pediatric artificial intelligence.","authorString":"Sezgin E, Boch S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sezgin E","firstName":"Emre","lastName":"Sezgin","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Biobehavioral Health, The Abigail Wexner Research Institute at Nationwide Children's Hospital, Columbus, OH, United States."},{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, The Ohio State University College of Medicine, Columbus, OH, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Boch S","firstName":"Samantha","lastName":"Boch","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1757-1662"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Child Health Equity and Outcomes Research, The Abigail Wexner Research Institute at Nationwide Children's Hospital, Columbus, OH, United States."},{"affiliation":"Center for Nursing Excellence, Nationwide Children's Hospital, Columbus, OH, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1757-1662"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1800047","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) presents a transformative opportunity for pediatric healthcare, promising advancements in early diagnosis, personalized treatment, and operational efficiency. However, the unique developmental, physiological, and ethical considerations inherent to children necessitate a specialized approach to AI integration. This study introduces the Pediatric AI Readiness (PAIR) Framework-a pediatric-focused implementation-readiness checklist intended to support clinicians, health system leaders, regulators, and developers as they plan, evaluate, and operationalize AI tools in pediatric settings. PAIR complements existing AI development, evaluation, and reporting guidance by focusing on institutional and workflow readiness for real-world use. It organizes readiness considerations across seven domains: ethics and governance, population and data representativeness, validation and testing, workflow integration, economic and sustainability analysis, low-resource adaptability, and transparent reporting. By prioritizing child-centric design, rigorous validation, and collaborative governance, the PAIR framework aims to bridge the critical gap between AI innovation and its responsible application, fostering improved design and healthier futures for children.","affiliation":"Center for Biobehavioral Health, The Abigail Wexner Research Institute at Nationwide Children's Hospital, Columbus, OH, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Review","Children","Pediatrics","Readiness","Implementation Framework"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1800047"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13176136"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13176136?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-18","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-18","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-19","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-01"},{"id":"41019285","source":"MED","pmid":"41019285","pmcid":"PMC12460403","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12460403"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2025.1653631","title":"A systematic review of ethical considerations of large language models in healthcare and medicine.","authorString":"Fareed M, Fatima M, Uddin J, Ahmed A, Sattar MA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Fareed M","firstName":"Muhammad","lastName":"Fareed","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Riphah School of Computing & Innovation, Riphah International University, Islamabad, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Fatima M","firstName":"Madeeha","lastName":"Fatima","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Riphah School of Computing & Innovation, Riphah International University, Islamabad, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Uddin J","firstName":"Jamal","lastName":"Uddin","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Riphah School of Computing & Innovation, Riphah International University, Islamabad, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Ahmed A","firstName":"Adeel","lastName":"Ahmed","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Riphah School of Computing & Innovation, Riphah International University, Islamabad, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Sattar MA","firstName":"Muhammad Awais","lastName":"Sattar","initials":"MA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2431-8182"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, Electrical and Space Engineering, Luleå University of Technology, Luleå, Sweden."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2431-8182"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"7","journalIssueId":3897144,"dateOfPublication":"2025 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"1653631","abstractText":"The rapid integration of large language models (LLMs) into healthcare offers significant potential for improving diagnosis, treatment planning, and patient engagement. However, it also presents serious ethical challenges that remain incompletely addressed. In this review, we analyzed 27 peer-reviewed studies published between 2017 and 2025 across four major open-access databases using strict eligibility criteria, robust synthesis methods, and established guidelines to explicitly examine the ethical aspects of deploying LLMs in clinical settings. We explore four key aspects, including the main ethical issues arising from the use of LLMs in healthcare, the prevalent model architectures employed in ethical analyses, the healthcare application domains that are most frequently scrutinized, and the publication and bibliographic patterns characterizing this literature. Our synthesis reveals that bias and fairness ( n=7 , 25.9%) are the most frequently discussed concerns, followed by safety, reliability, transparency, accountability, and privacy, and that the GPT family predominates ( n=14 , 51.8%) among examined models. While privacy protection and bias mitigation received notable attention in the literature, no existing review has systematically addressed the comprehensive ethical issues surrounding LLMs. Most previous studies focus narrowly on specific clinical subdomains and lack a comprehensive methodology. As a systematic mapping of open-access literature, this synthesis identifies dominant ethical patterns, but it is not exhaustive of all ethical work on LLMs in healthcare. We also synthesize identified challenges, outline future research directions and include a provisional ethical integration framework to guide clinicians, developers, and policymakers in the responsible integration of LLMs into clinical workflows.","affiliation":"Riphah School of Computing & Innovation, Riphah International University, Islamabad, Pakistan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","systematic-review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Bias","Privacy","Fairness","Deep Learning","Bioethical Issues","Artificial Intelligence (Ai)","Chatgpt","Large Language Models (Llms)"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2025.1653631"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12460403"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12460403?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":12,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-09-29","dateOfCreation":"2025-09-29","firstIndexDate":"2025-09-30","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-09-27","dateOfRevision":"2025-10-01","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-09-11","firstPublicationDate":"2025-09-11"},{"id":"41799484","source":"MED","pmid":"41799484","pmcid":"PMC12963277","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12963277"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpubh.2026.1769451","title":"Construction of a hospital intelligent integrity supervision platform: digital and intelligent practices for enhancing ethical governance in healthcare.","authorString":"Chen X.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chen X","firstName":"Xiaofei","lastName":"Chen","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Discipline Inspection and Supervision Office, Hangzhou Xixi Hospital, Hangzhou, Zhejiang, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4110762,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Public Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Public Health","nlmid":"101616579","essn":"2296-2565","issn":"2296-2565"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1769451","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>The digital transformation of governance in public hospitals is crucial for enhancing integrity and compliance. This study investigates the design, implementation, and impact of an Intelligent Integrity Supervision Platform in a hospital setting, aiming to provide a replicable model for data-driven ethical governance.<h4>Methods</h4>A case study approach was employed at Hangzhou Xixi Hospital, combining system analysis with outcome evaluation. The platform integrated multi-source data and established real-time early-warning mechanisms. Quantitative data on integrity incidents, medical disputes, and patient satisfaction were collected from quarterly reports (2024-2025) and analyzed to compare pre- and post-implementation periods.<h4>Results</h4>Following the platform's launch, integrity risk incidents decreased stepwise, achieving a 100% reduction (zero incidents) in the second half of 2025 compared to the baseline. Medical disputes fell by 75% year-on-year. Patient satisfaction metrics improved, including an increase in the platform complaint resolution rate from 95.1% to 97.3% and a 19.7% decline in discharge follow-up issue rates.<h4>Discussion</h4>The platform demonstrates the efficacy of digital-intelligent systems in transforming ethical governance by enabling proactive risk prevention, standardizing operations, and fostering accountability. It aligns with global efforts to leverage technology for anti-corruption in healthcare. The study concludes that such platforms serve as strategic tools for systemic governance reform, contributing to both institutional integrity and patient safety.","affiliation":"Discipline Inspection and Supervision Office, Hangzhou Xixi Hospital, Hangzhou, Zhejiang, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Hospitals, Public","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"OG","qualifierName":"organization & administration","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Management"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Patient Satisfaction"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"China"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Clinical Governance"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Intelligent Systems"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Data integration","Smart Hospital","Digital Governance","Intelligent Supervision Platform","Compliance Risk Management","Ethical Healthcare","Healthcare Integrity"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1769451"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12963277"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12963277?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-10","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-20"},{"id":"42259977","source":"MED","pmid":"42259977","pmcid":"PMC13246820","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13246820"]},"doi":"10.1007/s10072-026-09165-3","title":"Telemedicine documentation in neurology and telestroke: a global scoping review.","authorString":"de Andrade JBC, Novoa CG, Pisa IT, Carneiro TS, de Oliveira NS, Rodriguez CE, Mendes GNN.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"de Andrade JBC","firstName":"João Brainer Clares","lastName":"de Andrade","initials":"JBC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Neurology and Health Informatics, Federal University of Sao Paulo, Rua Botucatu, 862 Vila Clementino, Sao Paulo, CEP 04021-001, SP, Brazil. joao.brainer@unifesp.br."},{"affiliation":"Academic Research Organization, Hospital Israelita Albert Einstein, Sao Paulo, Brazil. joao.brainer@unifesp.br."},{"affiliation":"Bioengineering Laborator, Institute of Aeronautics Technology, São José dos Campos, São Paulo, Brazil. joao.brainer@unifesp.br."}]}},{"fullName":"Novoa CG","firstName":"Claudia Galindo","lastName":"Novoa","initials":"CG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Neurology and Health Informatics, Federal University of Sao Paulo, Rua Botucatu, 862 Vila Clementino, Sao Paulo, CEP 04021-001, SP, Brazil."}]}},{"fullName":"Pisa IT","firstName":"Ivan Torres","lastName":"Pisa","initials":"IT","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Neurology and Health Informatics, Federal University of Sao Paulo, Rua Botucatu, 862 Vila Clementino, Sao Paulo, CEP 04021-001, SP, Brazil."}]}},{"fullName":"Carneiro TS","firstName":"Thiago S","lastName":"Carneiro","initials":"TS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Neurocritical Care, University of Florida, Gainesville, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"de Oliveira NS","firstName":"Nathalia Souza","lastName":"de Oliveira","initials":"NS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, University Center São Camilo, São Paulo, Brazil."}]}},{"fullName":"Rodriguez CE","firstName":"Carlos Eduardo","lastName":"Rodriguez","initials":"CE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, University Center São Camilo, São Paulo, Brazil."}]}},{"fullName":"Mendes GNN","firstName":"George N Nunes","lastName":"Mendes","initials":"GNN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Neurology, Centre Hospitalier de I'Universite de Montreal, Montreal, QC, Canada."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"47","journalIssueId":4209678,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Neurological sciences : official journal of the Italian Neurological Society and of the Italian Society of Clinical Neurophysiology","medlineAbbreviation":"Neurol Sci","isoabbreviation":"Neurol Sci","nlmid":"100959175","essn":"1590-3478","issn":"1590-1874"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"556","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>The rapid global expansion of telemedicine has created regulatory heterogeneity affecting documentation requirements, with particular implications for neurological services where documentation demands differ from general consultations.<h4>Objectives</h4>To systematically examine documentation requirements for telemedicine across global jurisdictions, analyze convergence and divergence in regulatory approaches, evaluate specialty-specific requirements for neurology and telestroke, and explore liability frameworks for teleconsultations.<h4>Methods</h4>Scoping review following PRISMA-ScR framework. Comprehensive searches of academic databases and grey literature through August 2025, examining sources published, enacted, or updated after January 1, 2020. A composite regulatory intensity score was developed to enable systematic comparison across jurisdictions.<h4>Results</h4>Analysis of 147 regulatory sources across 52 jurisdictions revealed universal requirements for documentation elements while demonstrating substantial variation in consent documentation, data retention, electronic prescribing, and platform regulation. Regulatory intensity scores categorized jurisdictions across the full spectrum, with higher scores associated with more comprehensive documentation requirements.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Telemedicine has transitioned from emergency contingency to regulated permanent care globally, with convergence on core principles but meaningful divergence in implementation specifics. Interoperability and mutual recognition rather than uniformity should guide regulatory harmonization efforts.","affiliation":"Department of Neurology and Health Informatics, Federal University of Sao Paulo, Rua Botucatu, 862 Vila Clementino, Sao Paulo, CEP 04021-001, SP, Brazil. joao.brainer@unifesp.br.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Scoping Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Universidade Federal De São Paulo","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Neurology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"LJ","qualifierName":"legislation & jurisprudence","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Telemedicine","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"LJ","qualifierName":"legislation & jurisprudence","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Documentation","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Stroke","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Regulation","Neurology","Documentation","Telemedicine","Health Policy","Medical Informatics","Global Health","Telestroke"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10072-026-09165-3"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13246820"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13246820?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-08","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-09","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-09"},{"id":"41093629","source":"MED","pmid":"41093629","pmcid":"PMC12530383","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12530383"]},"doi":"10.1136/bmjhci-2025-101640","title":"Bridging generative AI and healthcare practice: insights from the GenAI Health Hackathon at Hospital Clínic de Barcelona.","authorString":"Frid S, Bassegoda O, Camacho Mahamud MA, Sanjuan G, Armengol de la Hoz MÁ, Celi L, Cano Franco I, Anmella G, Cuñat López T, Arellano AL, Leguízamo-Martínez LM, Mezquita L, Peñafiel Macías PA, Gallardo-Pizarro A, González Colom R, Renú Jornet A, Bracons Cucó G, Borrat Frigola X.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Frid S","firstName":"Santiago","lastName":"Frid","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8400-5770"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Informatics Service, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain frid@clinic.cat."},{"affiliation":"Clinical Foundations, Universitat de Barcelona Facultat de Medicina i Ciencies de la Salut, Barcelona, Spain."},{"affiliation":"Multimodal Imaging Biomarkers, Fundació de Recerca Clínic Barcelona-Institut d'Investigacions Biomèdiques August Pi i Sunyer, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Bassegoda O","firstName":"Octavi","lastName":"Bassegoda","initials":"O","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6036-6026"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Liver Intensive Care, Liver Unit, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Camacho Mahamud MA","firstName":"Maria Araceli","lastName":"Camacho Mahamud","initials":"MA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-1205-3288"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Strategy and Planning, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Sanjuan G","firstName":"Gemma","lastName":"Sanjuan","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1946-4345"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"IT Department, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Armengol de la Hoz MÁ","firstName":"Miguel Ángel","lastName":"Armengol de la Hoz","initials":"MÁ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7012-2973"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Big Data Department, PMC-FPS, Regional Government of Andalusia Ministry of Health and Consumer Affairs, Seville, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Celi L","firstName":"Leo","lastName":"Celi","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6712-6626"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Health Sciences and Tech, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Boston, Massachusetts, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Cano Franco I","firstName":"Isaac","lastName":"Cano Franco","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2938-7459"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Fundació de Recerca Clínic Barcelona-Institut d'Investigacions Biomèdiques August Pi i Sunyer, Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Anmella G","firstName":"Gerard","lastName":"Anmella","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6798-4054"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Psychology, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."},{"affiliation":"Bipolar and Depressive Disorders Unit, Digital Innovation Group, IDIBAPS, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Cuñat López T","firstName":"Tomas","lastName":"Cuñat López","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6475-9230"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Anesthesiology Service, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Arellano AL","firstName":"Ana Lucía","lastName":"Arellano","initials":"AL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8601-3504"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Fundació de Recerca Clínic Barcelona-Institut d'Investigacions Biomèdiques August Pi i Sunyer, Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."},{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical Pharmacology, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Leguízamo-Martínez LM","firstName":"Lina María","lastName":"Leguízamo-Martínez","initials":"LM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4523-7947"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Fundació de Recerca Clínic Barcelona-Institut d'Investigacions Biomèdiques August Pi i Sunyer, Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."},{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical Pharmacology, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Mezquita L","firstName":"Laura","lastName":"Mezquita","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0936-7338"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Oncology Service, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Peñafiel Macías PA","firstName":"Petter Axcell","lastName":"Peñafiel Macías","initials":"PA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-9917-9726"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Informatics Service, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Gallardo-Pizarro A","firstName":"Antonio","lastName":"Gallardo-Pizarro","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1517-4073"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Infectious Diseases Service, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"González Colom R","firstName":"Ruben","lastName":"González Colom","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7330-9621"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Fundació de Recerca Clínic Barcelona-Institut d'Investigacions Biomèdiques August Pi i Sunyer, Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Renú Jornet A","firstName":"Arturo","lastName":"Renú Jornet","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9169-6856"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Stroke Unit, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Bracons Cucó G","firstName":"Guillem","lastName":"Bracons Cucó","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1274-7403"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Informatics Service, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Mathematics and Statistics, Universitat Politecnica de Catalunya, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Borrat Frigola X","firstName":"Xavier","lastName":"Borrat Frigola","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9348-9284"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Informatics, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."},{"affiliation":"Clinical Foundations Department, Universitat de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6712-6626"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7330-9621"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8400-5770"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8601-3504"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9169-6856"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1946-4345"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4523-7947"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6036-6026"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6475-9230"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6798-4054"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7012-2973"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9348-9284"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0936-7338"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1274-7403"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1517-4073"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2938-7459"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-9917-9726"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-1205-3288"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"32","journalIssueId":4029143,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-10-01","journal":{"title":"BMJ health & care informatics","medlineAbbreviation":"BMJ Health Care Inform","isoabbreviation":"BMJ Health Care Inform","nlmid":"101745500","essn":"2632-1009","issn":"2632-1009"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"e101640","abstractText":"<h4>Objectives</h4>To describe the implementation of a multidisciplinary, ethically grounded hackathon as a model to develop and evaluate generative AI (GenAI) solutions for real-world clinical challenges within a hospital setting.<h4>Methods</h4>The GenAI Health Hackathon (GAHH) organised at Hospital Clínic de Barcelona included 13 challenges were selected via an internal call based on clinical impact, feasibility and data availability. Participants accessed anonymised real-world data through a secure cloud environment. Teams employed large language models and retrieval-augmented generation to build prototypes addressing tasks such as clinical text structuring, decision support and workflow automation. Human-in-the-loop validation, explainability and regulatory safeguards were emphasised.<h4>Results</h4>The hackathon yielded multiple AI prototypes tested on real data. Results varied: entity recognition reached 90.5% accuracy, summarisation >90% clinician concordance and nutritional models achieved F1 scores of 0.75-0.93. Lower scores (F1<0.52, Jaccard Index <0.4) were seen in complex reasoning or multilingual tasks. Bias was explored in 10 projects, with mitigations such as stratified sampling, prompt tuning, disclaimers and expert oversight. A transferable framework was proposed to replicate responsible GenAI hackathons in clinical contexts.<h4>Discussion</h4>Interdisciplinary collaboration and real-world testing proved essential for aligning GenAI with clinical needs. The hackathon revealed challenges in bias, evaluation and integration but offered a transferable framework for responsible innovation under General Data Protection Regulation and the European Union Artificial Intelligence Act.<h4>Conclusions</h4>The GAHH demonstrated that GenAI can be safely and effectively applied in healthcare with rigorous governance and interdisciplinary collaboration, offering a scalable model for responsible AI innovation.","affiliation":"Clinical Informatics Service, Hospital Clínic de Barcelona, Barcelona, Spain frid@clinic.cat.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Delivery of Health Care"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Spain"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjhci-2025-101640"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12530383"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12530383?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-10-15","dateOfCreation":"2025-10-15","firstIndexDate":"2025-10-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-10-22","dateOfRevision":"2025-10-18","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-10-15","firstPublicationDate":"2025-10-15"},{"id":"42211706","source":"MED","pmid":"42211706","pmcid":"PMC13212051","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13212051"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpubh.2026.1800597","title":"COVID-19 legislative response and challenges in Republic of Korea.","authorString":"Shin HY, Ki M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Shin HY","firstName":"Hyun-Young","lastName":"Shin","initials":"HY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Family Medicine, Seoul St. Mary's Hospital, College of Medicine, The Catholic University of Korea, Seoul, Republic of Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Ki M","firstName":"Moran","lastName":"Ki","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Cancer Center, Goyang-si, Republic of Korea."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4110762,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Public Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Public Health","nlmid":"101616579","essn":"2296-2565","issn":"2296-2565"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1800597","abstractText":"This narrative review systematically analyzes the longitudinal evolution of South Korea's legal and institutional frameworks from the 2015 MERS outbreak through the COVID-19 pandemic. The study introduces the \"Pandemic Response Pentad,\" an original conceptual model positioning Legislation as the foundational core, mediated by Governance, to drive three interconnected field operations. Based on this framework, Korea's infectious disease control system is evaluated across four operational domains: (1) governance reform and personnel structure enhancement, (2) epidemiological response capabilities and data utilization, (3) medical response system, including vaccination programs and supply stockpiling, and (4) social response mechanisms, encompassing social distancing and the protection of vulnerable populations. While rapid legislative enactments enabled effective disease control, they also generated profound ethical tensions regarding fundamental human rights. To mitigate these unintended socioeconomic consequences, Korea institutionalized extensive statutory compensation mechanisms for healthcare facilities, small business owners, and vaccine injuries. The findings highlight that advancing national research infrastructure and establishing policy-oriented think tanks for future pandemic preparedness strictly require proactive legislative backing. Ultimately, this study provides valuable insights into the critical interplay between legal mandates and institutional resilience in global health crisis management.","affiliation":"Department of Family Medicine, Seoul St. Mary's Hospital, College of Medicine, The Catholic University of Korea, Seoul, Republic of Korea.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Communicable Disease Control","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"LJ","qualifierName":"legislation & jurisprudence","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Health Policy","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"LJ","qualifierName":"legislation & jurisprudence","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Republic of Korea","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Pandemics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"COVID-19","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"PC","qualifierName":"prevention & control","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"SARS-CoV-2"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pandemic Preparedness"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Legislation","Pandemic","Public Health Policy","Governance","Preparedness","Covid-19"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1800597"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212051"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212051?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-17","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-13"},{"id":"42232959","source":"MED","pmid":"42232959","pmcid":"PMC13222949","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13222949"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpubh.2026.1836281","title":"The role of digital pharmaceutical governance in alleviating health inequality: a case study of the \"Sanya Model\" for centralized regional prescription review.","authorString":"Jia X, Xie J, Chai F, Jiang H, Shi R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Jia X","firstName":"Xu","lastName":"Jia","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of IT & Data Management of West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xie J","firstName":"Jun","lastName":"Xie","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of IT & Data Management of West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."},{"affiliation":"Sanya People's Hospital, Sanya, China."},{"affiliation":"West China (Sanya) Hospital, Sichuan University, Sanya, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chai F","firstName":"Fang","lastName":"Chai","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sanya People's Hospital, Sanya, China."},{"affiliation":"West China (Sanya) Hospital, Sichuan University, Sanya, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Jiang H","firstName":"HaoBo","lastName":"Jiang","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sanya People's Hospital, Sanya, China."},{"affiliation":"West China (Sanya) Hospital, Sichuan University, Sanya, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shi R","firstName":"Rui","lastName":"Shi","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of IT & Data Management of West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4110762,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Public Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Public Health","nlmid":"101616579","essn":"2296-2565","issn":"2296-2565"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1836281","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Irrational drug use poses a persistent challenge to healthcare quality and economic efficiency. In the context of China's \"Tight-knit Urban Medical Group\" reform, this study analyzes a digitized pharmaceutical governance model designed to bridge the gap in pharmaceutical care between tertiary hospitals and primary healthcare facilities. We aimed to analyze the implementation and public health correlation of a Centralized E-prescription Review (CEPR) platform within a regional medical network.<h4>Methods</h4>A retrospective, observational real-world study was conducted in Sanya, China. We implemented a CEPR platform utilizing a dual-layered cloud architecture and non-invasive data acquisition (XTL) to integrate regional drug metadata. The system incorporates an AI-driven engine fusing Large Language Models (LLMs) with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to provide clinical decision support. A total of 441,327 prescriptions were analyzed over a 12-month longitudinal period. The Cochran-Armitage trend test was employed to evaluate temporal shifts in prescription safety, economic efficiency, and service homogeneity.<h4>Results</h4>Following the implementation of the CEPR platform, the initial prescription pass rate at primary healthcare institutions rose significantly from 75.00 to 95.12%, and a 99.30% pharmacist intervention success rate was achieved across more than 16,000 intercepted high-risk orders. Regarding economic efficiency, the average cost per outpatient visit decreased by 4.58%, while inpatient drug costs dropped by 23.5%, accompanied by the implementation of a technology-based integrity oversight mechanism. Notably, the regional irrational prescription rate over 12 consecutive months of operation decreased markedly from 11.92 to 7.35% (<i>χ<sup>2</sup></i>  = 328.42, <i>p</i> < 0.001), demonstrating a robust temporal association between platform deployment and quality improvement.<h4>Conclusion</h4>The CEPR model, characterized by the \"Sanya Model,\" serves as a digital lever for enhancing the accessibility and homogeneity of high-quality pharmaceutical services. By shifting medication safety from a site-specific resource to a standardized public service, this framework supports the \"Three medical linkage\" (San Yi Lian Dong, i.e., the integrated reform of medical services, health insurance, and pharmaceutical systems) collaborative reform. These findings provide a scalable governance paradigm for improving public health resilience in regions with unevenly distributed medical resources.","affiliation":"Department of IT & Data Management of West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article","Observational Study"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Retrospective Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Primary Health Care"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"China"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Healthcare Disparities"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Electronic Prescribing"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Health Equality","Real-world Evidence","Centralised E-prescription Review","Digital Pharmaceutical Governance","Tight-knit Urban Medical Group"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1836281"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13222949"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13222949?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-07","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-03","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-04","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-18","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-18"},{"id":"41148218","source":"MED","pmid":"41148218","pmcid":"PMC13065136","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13065136"]},"doi":"10.34067/kid.0000001037","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Critical Care Nephrology: Current Applications, Emerging Techniques, and Challenges to Clinical Integration.","authorString":"Cheungpasitporn W, Thongprayoon C, Kashani K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cheungpasitporn W","firstName":"Wisit","lastName":"Cheungpasitporn","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9954-9711"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota."}]}},{"fullName":"Thongprayoon C","firstName":"Charat","lastName":"Thongprayoon","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8313-3604"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota."}]}},{"fullName":"Kashani K","firstName":"Kianoush","lastName":"Kashani","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2184-3683"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota."},{"affiliation":"Division of Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9954-9711"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8313-3604"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2184-3683"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"7","journalIssueId":4164147,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Kidney360","medlineAbbreviation":"Kidney360","isoabbreviation":"Kidney360","nlmid":"101766381","essn":"2641-7650","issn":"2641-7650"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"664-677","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI), including machine learning, deep learning, reinforcement learning, and generative AI, has the potential to advance critical care nephrology (CCN) by enhancing prediction accuracy, improving diagnostic capabilities, supporting clinical decision making, and streamlining workflow processes. Current applications in CCN include AKI prediction, nephrotoxin surveillance, intradialytic hypotension forecasting, and AI-guided dialysis and continuous KRT management, with performance often exceeding traditional models. However, the effect on patient-centered outcomes such as mortality, dialysis dependence, and cost-effectiveness remains uncertain. Emerging techniques, such as conformal prediction for calibrated risk estimates, causal inference for intervention modeling, and reinforcement learning for adaptive ultrafiltration, show promise in enhancing reliability, interpretability, and individualized care. Generative AI and large language models extend these applications to clinical documentation, reasoning, and patient education, while raising new challenges, including hallucinations, regulatory oversight, and clinician trust. Persistent barriers such as data heterogeneity, limited external validation, alert fatigue, and economic constraints hinder broad adoption. This review synthesizes the current evidence and outlines four priorities for advancing AI in CCN: ( 1 ) rigorous multicenter validation focused on clinical outcomes, ( 2 ) integration of uncertainty quantification and causal modeling into AI tools, ( 3 ) development of clinician-centered interfaces that minimize cognitive load, and ( 4 ) establishment of transparent, adaptive regulatory and governance frameworks. Realizing the promise of AI in CCN will require multidisciplinary collaboration, fairness and generalizability testing, and sustainable implementation strategies that align technologic innovation with measurable improvements in patient care.","affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Critical Care","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Nephrology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Acute Kidney Injury","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Clinical Decision-Making"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Deep Learning"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Dialysis","acute renal failure","Aki","Acute Kidney Failure"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.34067/KID.0000001037"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13065136"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13065136?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":3,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-30","dateOfCreation":"2025-10-28","firstIndexDate":"2025-10-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-10-28","firstPublicationDate":"2025-10-28"},{"id":"42014474","source":"MED","pmid":"42014474","pmcid":"PMC13287483","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13287483"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02508-6","title":"Tracing the Pen: Electronic Health Records Amid the Rise of Generative AI.","authorString":"Nargesi AA, You JG, Bitterman DS, Succi MD, Mishuris RG, Poon EG, Landman AB.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Nargesi AA","firstName":"Arash A","lastName":"Nargesi","initials":"AA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Cardiovascular Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Clinical Informatics, Mass General Brigham, Somerville, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Broad Institute of MIT and Harvard, Cambridge, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"You JG","firstName":"Jacqueline G","lastName":"You","initials":"JG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of General Internal Medicine and Primary Care, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Digital, Mass General Brigham, Somerville, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Bitterman DS","firstName":"Danielle S","lastName":"Bitterman","initials":"DS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Digital, Mass General Brigham, Somerville, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"AI in Medicine Program, Mass General Brigham, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Succi MD","firstName":"Marc D","lastName":"Succi","initials":"MD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Innovation, Mass General Brigham, Somerville, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Medically Engineered Solutions in Healthcare Incubator, Innovation in Operations Research Center (MESH IO), Massachusetts General Hospital, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Mishuris RG","firstName":"Rebecca G","lastName":"Mishuris","initials":"RG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of General Internal Medicine and Primary Care, Department of Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Digital, Mass General Brigham, Somerville, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Poon EG","firstName":"Eric G","lastName":"Poon","initials":"EG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, NC, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biostatistics & Bioinformatics, Duke University School of Medicine, Durham, NC, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Landman AB","firstName":"Adam B","lastName":"Landman","initials":"AB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Brown University Health, Providence, RI, USA. Adam.landman@brownhealth.org."},{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Brown University, Providence, RI, USA. Adam.landman@brownhealth.org."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4171893,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738","essn":"2398-6352","issn":"2398-6352"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"482","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) are poised to transform physician interactions with electronic health records (EHRs) by assisting clinical documentation, drafting preliminary diagnostic reports, and supporting patient communication. While LLMs reduce administrative burden, their integration in clinical workflows introduces the risk of blending AI- and human-generated content within EHRs. This perspective reviews technological and policy solutions to ensure traceability of AI-generated content in EHRs to preserve clinical integrity.","affiliation":"Division of Cardiovascular Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Boston, MA, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"T15LM007092","agency":"National Library of Medicine/National Institutes of Health","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"T32HL007604","agency":"National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI) of the National Institutes of Health","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"T32HL007604","agency":"National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI) of the National Institutes of Health (NIH)","orderIn":0},{"agency":"the American Cancer Society and American Society for Radiation Oncology","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Woods Foundation","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health National Cancer Institute","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02508-6"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13287483"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13287483?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-22","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-21"},{"id":"42273287","source":"MED","pmid":"42273287","pmcid":"PMC13248940","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13248940"]},"doi":"10.1093/jlb/lsag003","title":"Chat M.D.","authorString":"Cortez N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cortez N","firstName":"Nathan","lastName":"Cortez","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Southern Methodist University (SMU) Dedman School of Law, Dallas, TX, USA."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"13","journalIssueId":4126395,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan-Jun","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of law and the biosciences","medlineAbbreviation":"J Law Biosci","isoabbreviation":"J Law Biosci","nlmid":"101633120","essn":"2053-9711","issn":"2053-9711"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"lsag003","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) such as Claude and ChatGPT are the most powerful artificial intelligence (AI) systems ever created, and they are being used to diagnose and treat patients. But LLMs have been shown to be unreliable, unpredictable, and unsafe on occasion. New AI guidelines recommend hundreds of standards, such as 'transparency', 'trustworthiness', and 'safety'. But there is deep uncertainty whether these are sufficient. The literature focuses mostly on which standards best suit AI models, not on how to transmute standards into law. This article does that by considering AI guidelines as a starting point, then evaluating whether existing frameworks for ensuring quality in medicine might form the basis for AI governance. Along the way, the article identifies emerging areas of consensus, lingering questions, and lessons from other areas of law. This article suggests that we should not only treat LLMs like nascent medical professionals who must meet minimum standards of competence and responsibility, but also subject them to measurable, product-like standards of safety and performance. When paired with reimbursement incentives and legal liability, LLMs will be treated on par with others who diagnose and treat US patients. This article offers a path to genuine oversight of LLMs in medicine.","affiliation":"Southern Methodist University (SMU) Dedman School of Law, Dallas, TX, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Regulation","Medicine","Liability","Fda","Chatbots"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/jlb/lsag003"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13248940"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13248940?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-11","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-11","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-11","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-11","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},{"id":"41738015","source":"MED","pmid":"41738015","pmcid":"PMC12926588","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12926588"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.jot.2026.101050","title":"Medical large language models and systems in the clinical application of spinal diseases: Current status, challenges, and future prospects.","authorString":"Tang W, Chen R, Long X, Yu D, Zhao S, Chen B.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tang W","firstName":"Wenyan","lastName":"Tang","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopedic Surgery, The First Affiliated Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, 310000, China."},{"affiliation":"School of Mathematical Sciences, Zhejiang Normal University, Jinhua, Zhejiang, 321004, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen R","firstName":"Ruizhi","lastName":"Chen","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopedic Surgery, The First Affiliated Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, 310000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Long X","firstName":"Xiao","lastName":"Long","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopedic Surgery, The First Affiliated Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, 310000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yu D","firstName":"Dongdong","lastName":"Yu","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopedic Surgery, The First Affiliated Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, 310000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhao S","firstName":"Shen","lastName":"Zhao","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Intelligent Systems Engineering, Sun Yat-sen University, Guangzhou, Guangdong, 510080, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen B","firstName":"Bin","lastName":"Chen","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopedic Surgery, The First Affiliated Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, 310000, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"57","journalIssueId":4131475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of orthopaedic translation","medlineAbbreviation":"J Orthop Translat","isoabbreviation":"J Orthop Translat","nlmid":"101625127","essn":"2214-0328","issn":"2214-031X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"101050","abstractText":"Large Language Models (LLMs), represented by the Generative Pretrained Transformer (GPT), are profoundly transforming the healthcare sector. Spine medicine, a discipline heavily reliant on complex imaging data, detailed clinical records, and evidence-based medical practice, serves as an ideal testing ground for exploring and applying these advanced artificial intelligence technologies. It holds the promise of optimizing clinical workflows, enhancing the quality of diagnosis and treatment decisions, and improving patient communication. We systematically searched PubMed and Embase from January 2023 to September 2025 for studies investigating LLMs in spinal diseases. Original research articles published in English with a Journal Impact Factor (JIF) ≥ 3.0 were included. Reviews, case reports, animal studies, and non-orthopedic topics were excluded. Data from eligible studies were extracted and narratively synthesized. This review aims to systematically and comprehensively examine the current state of clinical applications of medical large models and related intelligent systems in the field of spinal diseases. The focus is on analyzing their core technical pathways, specific clinical application scenarios, and their medical value, and performance evaluation results, thereby identifying current opportunities and key challenges. Furthermore, it anticipates future developments, from leveraging general-purpose models to constructing specialized models based on high-quality, large-scale, multimodal spine-specific datasets. <i>The translational potential of this article</i>: The translational potential of this article lies in its provision of a comprehensive roadmap and practical framework for implementing artificial intelligence in spinal surgery. It systematically synthesizes core application scenarios for large language models-including clinical documentation assistance and preoperative planning-while explicitly addressing four critical challenges requiring resolution for successful clinical integration: regulatory compliance, data privacy protection, algorithmic bias mitigation, and workflow integration. It establishes an actionable foundation for collaborative efforts among clinicians, developers, and policymakers to deploy safe, effective, and compliant AI tools in spinal care.","affiliation":"Department of Orthopedic Surgery, The First Affiliated Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, 310000, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Zhejiang University","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Spinal Diseases","Interdisciplinary Application","Multi Modal","Large Language Model","Retrieval-augmented Generation"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jot.2026.101050"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12926588"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12926588?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-18","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-18"},{"id":"41602313","source":"MED","pmid":"41602313","pmcid":"PMC12832696","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12832696"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2025.1725853","title":"Positive sentiments in early academic literature on DeepSeek: a cross-disciplinary mini review.","authorString":"He Y, Giangan A, Vu N, Watters C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"He Y","firstName":"Yuxing","lastName":"He","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Law, Bond University, Robina, QLD, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Giangan A","firstName":"Angie","lastName":"Giangan","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Independent researcher, Robina, QLD, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Vu N","firstName":"Nam","lastName":"Vu","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Logistics, Procurement and Supply Chain Management, Cranfield School of Management, Cranfield University, Cranfield, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Watters C","firstName":"Casey","lastName":"Watters","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Law, Bond University, Robina, QLD, Australia."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":3897209,"dateOfPublication":"2025 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"1725853","abstractText":"DeepSeek is a free and self-hostable large language model (LLM) that recently became the most downloaded app across 156 countries. As early academic literature on ChatGPT was predominantly critical of the model, this mini-review is interested in examining how DeepSeek is being evaluated across academic disciplines. The review analyzes available articles with DeepSeek in the title, abstract, or keywords, using the VADER sentiment analysis library. Due to limitations in comparing sentiment across languages, we excluded Chinese literature in our selection. We found that Computer Science, Engineering, and Medicine are the most prominent fields studying DeepSeek, showing an overall positive sentiment. Notably, Computer Science had the highest mean sentiment and the most positive articles. Other fields of interest included Mathematics, Business, and Environmental Science. While there is substantial academic interest in DeepSeek's practicality and performance, discussions on its political or ethical implications are limited in academic literature. In contrast to ChatGPT, where all early literature carried a negative sentiment, DeepSeek literature is mainly positive. This study enhances our understanding of DeepSeek's reception in the scientific community and suggests that further research could explore regional perspectives.","affiliation":"Faculty of Law, Bond University, Robina, QLD, Australia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Neural networks","Censorship","Natural Language Processing (Nlp)","Deep Learning","Large Language Models (Llm)","Chinese Ai","Deepseek"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2025.1725853"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12832696"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12832696?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-01-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-31","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-12"},{"id":"41924178","source":"MED","pmid":"41924178","pmcid":"PMC13035794","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13035794"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1772274","title":"Promises and challenges of applying large language models in the healthcare domain.","authorString":"Wang Q, Gong Z, Lai Z, Bu L, Dahlweid FM, Sun H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Wang Q","firstName":"Qingyu","lastName":"Wang","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Perceiving and Intelligent Systems, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."},{"affiliation":"Sino-European Joint Lab for Health Information Processing and Applications, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Gong Z","firstName":"Ziheng","lastName":"Gong","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Perceiving and Intelligent Systems, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."},{"affiliation":"Sino-European Joint Lab for Health Information Processing and Applications, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lai Z","firstName":"Zou","lastName":"Lai","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Perceiving and Intelligent Systems, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."},{"affiliation":"Sino-European Joint Lab for Health Information Processing and Applications, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Bu L","firstName":"Lina","lastName":"Bu","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Perceiving and Intelligent Systems, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."},{"affiliation":"Sino-European Joint Lab for Health Information Processing and Applications, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Dahlweid FM","firstName":"Fried-Michael","lastName":"Dahlweid","initials":"FM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sino-European Joint Lab for Health Information Processing and Applications, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."},{"affiliation":"Dedalus Healthcare, Milan, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Sun H","firstName":"Hong","lastName":"Sun","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Perceiving and Intelligent Systems, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."},{"affiliation":"Sino-European Joint Lab for Health Information Processing and Applications, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1772274","abstractText":"Large language models are rapidly moving from theoretical concepts to active clinical pilots. Current approaches diverge between general-purpose models, which adapt to healthcare via prompt engineering, and domain-specific models, which prioritize deep alignment with medical knowledge graphs to ensure safety. Despite reported benefits in documentation efficiency and diagnostic reasoning, significant challenges remain regarding hallucination, privacy, and the validity of evaluation metrics. This Mini Review synthesizes current evidence, contrasts these two modeling paradigms, highlights key controversies, and maps out future development routes including retrieval-augmented generation and agentic architectures.","affiliation":"Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Perceiving and Intelligent Systems, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Clinical Decision Support","Multimodal","Large Language Models","Model Hallucinations"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1772274"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13035794"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13035794?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-01","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-17"},{"id":"42354518","source":"MED","pmid":"42354518","doi":"10.3390/healthcare14121660","title":"Governing Generative AI for Healthy Ageing: A Normative Conceptual Framework for Societal Alignment, Epistemic Authority, and Value Convergence in Geriatric Care.","authorString":"Alves Ferreira JM, Tukaiev S, Giannouli V.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Alves Ferreira JM","firstName":"João Miguel","lastName":"Alves Ferreira","initials":"JM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9905-0849"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Pharmacology and Experimental Therapeutics, Faculty of Medicine, University of Coimbra, 3004-531 Coimbra, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"Coimbra Institute for Clinical and Biomedical Research (ICBR), Faculty of Medicine, University of Coimbra, 3004-531 Coimbra, Portugal."},{"affiliation":"Center for Innovative Biomedicine and Bio-Technology (CIBB), University of Coimbra, 3004-531 Coimbra, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Tukaiev S","firstName":"Sergii","lastName":"Tukaiev","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6342-1879"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Communication, Culture, and Society, Institute of Public Health, Università Della Svizzera Italiana, 6900 Lugano, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"Higher Institute of Science Education and Technology, Taras Shevchenko National University of Kyiv, 01033 Kyiv, Ukraine."}]}},{"fullName":"Giannouli V","firstName":"Vaitsa","lastName":"Giannouli","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2176-8986"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, Democritus University of Thrace, 68300 Didymoteicho, Greece."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9905-0849"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6342-1879"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2176-8986"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"12","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4221244,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525","essn":"2227-9032","issn":"2227-9032"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1660","abstractText":"<h4>Background/objectives</h4>Large language models (LLMs) and generative AI are rapidly being integrated into healthy ageing initiatives for tasks ranging from companionship and cognitive support to personalised health advice and reduction in social isolation among older adults. Current ethical discussions predominantly address bias, privacy, and accuracy, leaving unresolved three critical governance questions: How do LLM sentiments towards transformative technologies diverge from human values in ageing contexts? What epistemic status do LLM outputs hold when applied to geriatric care? When is trust in those outputs justified for older adults? And who bears responsibility when AI-informed decisions affect functional ability or well-being?<h4>Methods</h4>The framework was developed through normative conceptual analysis, synthesizing philosophical principles of medical knowledge and trust, ethical theories of responsibility, empirical evidence on LLM sentiment divergence, digital ageism, and applications of AI in geriatric care (structured searches in PubMed, PhilPapers, and relevant databases, January 2020-March 2026).<h4>Results</h4>The integrated framework produces (i) adaptation of SAIA for multidimensional evaluation of human-machine value convergence specific to healthy ageing values (functional ability, autonomy, dignity, equity); (ii) a four-tier classification of LLM outputs tailored to geriatric scenarios; (iii) conditions for warranted trust calibrated to age-related vulnerabilities such as cognitive decline and digital divide; and (iv) responsibility allocation via RACI models with testable hypotheses linking governance design to trust calibration and patient safety outcomes.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Without explicit societal alignment and epistemic governance, generative AI risks reinforcing benevolent ageism, automation bias, and responsibility gaps in healthy ageing. The 2025-2027 period offers a decisive window to shape institutional norms that place functional capacity, human dignity, and value convergence at the centre of AI deployment in geriatric care.","affiliation":"Institute of Pharmacology and Experimental Therapeutics, Faculty of Medicine, University of Coimbra, 3004-531 Coimbra, Portugal.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Healthy Ageing","Epistemic Authority","Automation Bias","Responsibility Gap","Digital Ageism","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Models","Geriatric Governance","Societal Ai Alignment","Warranted Trust"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14121660"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-11"},{"id":"41901113","source":"MED","pmid":"41901113","pmcid":"PMC13029413","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13029413"]},"doi":"10.3390/nu18060938","title":"Generative AI in Precision Nutrition: A Review of Current Developments and Future Directions.","authorString":"Abdur Rahman L, Dedousis V, Papathanail I, Poursoleymani R, Kafyra M, Kalafati IP, Mougiakakou SG.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Abdur Rahman L","firstName":"Lubnaa","lastName":"Abdur Rahman","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7271-2814"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School for Cellular and Biomedical Sciences, University of Bern, 3012 Bern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"ARTORG Center for Biomedical Engineering Research, University of Bern, 3008 Bern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Dedousis V","firstName":"Vasileios","lastName":"Dedousis","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-1439-9564"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School for Cellular and Biomedical Sciences, University of Bern, 3012 Bern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"ARTORG Center for Biomedical Engineering Research, University of Bern, 3008 Bern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Papathanail I","firstName":"Ioannis","lastName":"Papathanail","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7767-3831"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ARTORG Center for Biomedical Engineering Research, University of Bern, 3008 Bern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Poursoleymani R","firstName":"Rooholla","lastName":"Poursoleymani","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-4365-6782"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School for Cellular and Biomedical Sciences, University of Bern, 3012 Bern, Switzerland."},{"affiliation":"ARTORG Center for Biomedical Engineering Research, University of Bern, 3008 Bern, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Kafyra M","firstName":"Maria","lastName":"Kafyra","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-9665-0829"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nutrition and Dietetics, School of Health Science and Education, Harokopio University of Athens, 17676 Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Kalafati IP","firstName":"Ioanna Panagiota","lastName":"Kalafati","initials":"IP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5572-7491"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nutrition and Dietetics, School of Health Science and Education, Harokopio University of Athens, 17676 Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Mougiakakou SG","firstName":"Stavroula Georgia","lastName":"Mougiakakou","initials":"SG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6355-9982"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ARTORG Center for Biomedical Engineering Research, University of Bern, 3008 Bern, Switzerland."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5572-7491"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7271-2814"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6355-9982"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7767-3831"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-1439-9564"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-4365-6782"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-9665-0829"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4161783,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Nutrients","medlineAbbreviation":"Nutrients","isoabbreviation":"Nutrients","nlmid":"101521595","essn":"2072-6643","issn":"2072-6643"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"938","abstractText":"<b>Background:</b> Precision nutrition (PN) aims to personalize dietary guidance by accounting for inter-individual variability across biological, metabolic, lifestyle, and environmental factors influencing nutritional needs and health outcomes. While traditional Artificial Intelligence (AI) has advanced nutritional research through systems like automated dietary assessment, these models often operate rigidly. Generative AI (GenAI) introduces the capacity for adaptive interventions for enhanced PN. However, the scope and maturity of its applications remain insufficiently characterized. <b>Objective:</b> This review examined original works applying GenAI in PN, focusing on application, methodology, and limitations. <b>Methods:</b> A systematic search was conducted in PubMed, ACM Digital Library, and Scopus. Inclusion criteria focused on original works deploying GenAI models in PN contexts. Included works were further formally assessed based on data used, validation, transparency, bias, and security and privacy. <b>Results:</b> 21 eligible studies were identified, all published after 2024. The literature indicated a surge in large language model-based systems for personalized dietary recommendations, followed by applications in data foundation building and food effect understanding. A recurrent limitation was questionable evaluation on synthetic data and hallucinations, necessitating a human-expert-in-the-loop, especially in high-stakes clinical settings. Additionally, only 4 of 21 reviewed studies incorporated biological content or biological inputs, and fewer approached biologically grounded PN within implemented personalization workflows using metabolic and/or genomic variables. <b>Conclusions:</b> Although GenAI research in PN is expanding rapidly, most applications remain personalized at a user-preference level rather than including biological determinants. The need for standardized reporting, stronger genome-informed modeling, and consistent human-in-the-loop validation protocols is further highlighted to advance towards holistic PN.","affiliation":"Graduate School for Cellular and Biomedical Sciences, University of Bern, 3012 Bern, Switzerland.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"101080117","agency":"European Commission and the Swiss Confederation–State Secretariat for Education, Research and Innovation","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"101080117","agency":"European Commission and State Secretariat for Education, Research and Innovation (SERI)","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"10093560","agency":"UK Research and Innovation (UKRI) under the UK government’s Horizon Europe funding guarantee","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"101057730","agency":"European Commission and State Secretariat for Education, Research and Innovation (SERI)","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"101057730","agency":"European Commission and the Swiss Confederation–State Secretariat for Education, Research and Innovation","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"10106435","agency":"UK Research and Innovation (UKRI) under the UK government’s Horizon Europe funding guarantee","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Precision Medicine","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Personalized Nutrition","Precision Nutrition","Generative Artificial Intelligence"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/nu18060938"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13029413"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13029413?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":3,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-29","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-17"},{"id":"42137795","source":"MED","pmid":"42137795","pmcid":"PMC13168041","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13168041"]},"doi":"10.3389/fmicb.2026.1814993","title":"Know your scientist: KYC as biosecurity infrastructure.","authorString":"Feldman J, Feldman T, Anton AI.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Feldman J","firstName":"Jonathan","lastName":"Feldman","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Computing, Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, GA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Feldman T","firstName":"Tal","lastName":"Feldman","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Yale Law School, New Haven, CT, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Anton AI","firstName":"Annie I","lastName":"Anton","initials":"AI","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Computing, Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, GA, United States."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"17","journalIssueId":4112147,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in microbiology","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Microbiol","isoabbreviation":"Front Microbiol","nlmid":"101548977","essn":"1664-302X","issn":"1664-302X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1814993","abstractText":"Biological AI tools for protein design and structure prediction are advancing rapidly, creating dual-use risks that existing safeguards cannot adequately address. Current model-level restrictions, including keyword filtering, output screening, and content-based access denials, are fundamentally ill-suited to biology, where reliable function prediction remains beyond reach and novel threats evade detection by design. Because the full spectrum of risks cannot be managed by any single actor, effective oversight requires shared responsibility between research institutions and model hosts. Hence, we propose a three-tier Know Your Customer (KYC) framework, inspired by anti-money laundering (AML) practices in the financial sector, that augments existing approaches, supplementing content inspection with complementary layers of user verification and monitoring. Tier I leverages research institutions as trust anchors to vouch for affiliated researchers and assume responsibility for vetting. Tier II applies output screening through sequence homology searches and functional annotation. Tier III monitors behavioral patterns to detect anomalies inconsistent with declared research purposes. This layered approach preserves access for legitimate researchers while raising the cost of misuse through institutional accountability and traceability. The framework can be implemented immediately using existing institutional infrastructure, requiring no new legislation or regulatory mandates.","affiliation":"College of Computing, Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, GA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Biosecurity","Kyc","Access Control","Ai Governance","Biological Design Tools","Dual-use Risk"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2026.1814993"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13168041"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13168041?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-29"}]}}